window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-16"]=[{"id":"q2-259","q":"2:259","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:259 · The ruined town, the hundred years and the renewal of certainty","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 291–293","cairo":"I:291–293","source_page_first":291,"source_page_last":293,"ar":"أَوْ كَالَّذِي مَرَّ عَلى قَرْيَةٍ وَهِيَ خاوِيَةٌ عَلى عُرُوشِها قالَ أَنَّى يُحْيِي هذِهِ اللَّهُ بَعْدَ مَوْتِها فَأَماتَهُ اللَّهُ مِائَةَ عامٍ ثُمَّ بَعَثَهُ قالَ كَمْ لَبِثْتَ قالَ لَبِثْتُ يَوْماً أَوْ بَعْضَ يَوْمٍ قالَ بَلْ لَبِثْتَ مِائَةَ عامٍ فَانْظُرْ إِلى طَعامِكَ وَشَرابِكَ لَمْ يَتَسَنَّهْ وَانْظُرْ إِلى حِمارِكَ وَلِنَجْعَلَكَ آيَةً لِلنَّاسِ وَانْظُرْ إِلَى الْعِظامِ كَيْفَ نُنْشِزُها ثُمَّ نَكْسُوها لَحْماً فَلَمَّا تَبَيَّنَ لَهُ قالَ أَعْلَمُ أَنَّ اللَّهَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ (259)\nقلت: (أو) : عاطفة، و (كالذي) : معطوف على الموصول المجرور بإلى، أي: أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الَّذِي حَآجَّ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِى رَبِّهِ، وإلى مثل الذي مر على قرية. وإنما أدخل حرف التشبيه لأن المُنكر للإحياء كثيرٌ، والجاهل بكيفيته أكثر، بخلاف مدعي الربوبية فإنه قليل. وقيل: الكاف مزيدة، والتقدير: أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الَّذِي حاج وإلى الذي مرَّ، (أنَّى) :\nظرف ليُحيي، بمعنى: متى، أو حال بمعنى كيف، و (يتسنه) بمعنى يتغير، وأصله: يتسنن، فأبدلت النون الثالثة حرف علة. قال في الكافية:\nوثَالِثَ الأمثَالِ أبدِلَنْه يا ... نحو (تَظَنَّى خالدٌ تَظَنِّيَا)\nفصار تَسَنَّى ثم حُذفت للجازم، وأتى بهاء السَّكْت، وقفا ووصلا، كالعِوض من المحذوف، وقيل: من السَّنة، وهو التغير، فالهاء أصلية، و (لنجعلك) : معطوف على محذوف، أي: لتعتبر ولنجعلك آية للناس.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ألم ترَ يا محمد أيضاً إلى مثل الذي مَرَّ عَلى قَرْيَةٍ، وهو عزير، حَبْرُ بني إسرائيل. - وقيل: غيره- مرَّ على بيت المقدس حين خربها بُختنصر وَهِيَ خاوِيَةٌ ساقطة حيطانها عَلى عُرُوشِها أي: سقفها، وذلك بعد مائة سنة حتى سقطت العروش، ثم سقطت الحيطان عليها، فلما رآها خالية، وعظام الموتى فيها بالية، قالَ في نفسه: أَنَّى يُحْيِي هذِهِ اللَّهُ بَعْدَ مَوْتِها أي: متى يقع هذا. اعترافاً بالقصور عن معرفة طريق الإحياء، واستعظاماً لقدرة المحيي، إن كان القائل عزيراً، أو استبعاداً إن كان كافراً، فَأَماتَهُ اللَّهُ مِائَةَ عامٍ أي: ألبثه ميتاً مائة عام، ثُمَّ بَعَثَهُ بالإحياء، فقال له على لسان الملك، أو بلا واسطة: كَمْ لَبِثْتَ ميتاً؟ قالَ لَبِثْتُ يَوْماً أَوْ بَعْضَ يَوْمٍ، وذلك أنه مات ضحى وبعث بعد مائة عام قبل غروب الشمس، فقال قبل النظر إلى الشمس (يوماً) ، ثم التفت فرأى بقية منها، فقال: أَوْ بَعْضَ يَوْمٍ على الإضراب، قال له الحق جلّ جلاله: بَلْ لَبِثْتَ مِائَةَ عامٍ.\nوذلك أن عزيراً ذهب ليخترف(1) لأهله فجعل على حماره سَلة عنب وجَرَّة عصير. فلما مرَّ بتلك القرية ربط حماره، وجعل يتعجب من خرابها وخلائها بعد عمارتها، فقال في نفسه ما قال، فلطف الله به، وأراه كيفية الأحياء عياناً، فأماته مائة عام، حتى بليت عظام حماره وبقي العصير والعنب كأنه حين جنى وعصر فقال له جلّ جلاله: فَانْظُرْ إِلى طَعامِكَ وهو العنب، وَشَرابِكَ وهو العصير، لَمْ يَتَسَنَّهْ، أي: لم يتغير بمرور الزمان وطول المدة، وَانْظُرْ إِلى حِمارِكَ كيف تفرقت أوصاله، وبليت عظامه، فعلنا ذلك بك لتشاهد قدرتنا، وَلِنَجْعَلَكَ آيَةً لِلنَّاسِ بعدك، وَانْظُرْ إِلَى الْعِظامِ أي: عظام حمارك، كَيْفَ نُنْشِرُها، أي: نحييها، من نَشَرَ الله الموتى: أحياها. أو: كَيْفَ نُنْشِزُها بالزاي- أي: نرفع بعضها، ونركبه عليه، ثُمَّ نَكْسُوها لَحْماً.\nفنظر إلى العظام، فقام كلُّ عَظْم إلى موضعه، ثم كسى لحماً وجلداً، وجعل ينهق، فَلَمَّا تَبَيَّنَ لَهُ ما كان استغربه وأُشكل عليه قالَ أَعْلَمُ علم اليقين أَنَّ اللَّهَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ، أو فلما تبين له الحق، وهو قدرته تعالى على كل شيء، قال لنفسه: اعْلَمْ أَنَّ اللَّهَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ.\nرُوِيَ أنه أتى قومه على حماره، وقال أنا عزير، فكذبوه، فقرأ التوراة من حفظه، ولم يحفظها أحد قبله، فعرفوه بذلك، وقالوا: هو ابن الله- تعالى عن قولهم- وقيل: لما رجع إلى منزله- وكان شاباً- وجد أولاده شيوخاً، فإذا حدَّثهم بحديث قالوا: حديث مائة سنة. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: في هذه الآية والتي بعدها، الإشارة إلى الأمر بتربية اليقين والترقي فيه من علم اليقين إلى عين اليقين، فإن الروح ما دامت محجوبة بالوقوف مع الأسباب والعوائد، وبرؤية الحس والوقوف مع الوسائط، لم تخل من طوارق الشكوك والخواطر، فإذا انقطعت إلى ربها، وخرقت عوائد نفسها، كشف لها الحق تعالى عن أستار غيبه، وأطلعها على مكنونات سره، وكشف لها عن أسرار الملكوت، وأراها سنا الجبروت، فنظرت إلى قدرة الحي الذي لا يموت، وتمتعت بشهود الذات وأنوار الصفات، في هذه الحياة وبعد الممات، فحينئذٍ ينقطع عنها الشكوك والأوهام، وتتطهر من طوارق الخواطر، وتزول عنها الأمراض والأسقام.\nقال في الحِكَم: «كيف تخرق لك العوائد وأنت لم تخرق من نفسك العوائد» . فانظر إلى عزير ... ما أراه الحق قدرته عياناً حتى خرق له عوائده فأماته ثم أحياه، فكذلك أنت أيها المريد لا تطمع أن تخرق لك العوائد، فتشاهد قدرة الحق أو ذاته عياناً، حتى تموت عن حظوظك وهواك، ثم تحيا روحك وسرك، فحينئذٍ تشاهد أسرار ربك، ويكشف الأستار عن عين قلبك. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم ذكر الحقّ تعالى قصة خليله عليه السلام في طلبه رؤية عين القدرة في إحياء الموتى، ليترقى من علم اليقين إلى عين اليقين، فقال:","quran_ar":"أَوْ كَالَّذِي مَرَّ عَلى قَرْيَةٍ وَهِيَ خاوِيَةٌ عَلى عُرُوشِها قالَ أَنَّى يُحْيِي هذِهِ اللَّهُ بَعْدَ مَوْتِها فَأَماتَهُ اللَّهُ مِائَةَ عامٍ ثُمَّ بَعَثَهُ قالَ كَمْ لَبِثْتَ قالَ لَبِثْتُ يَوْماً أَوْ بَعْضَ يَوْمٍ قالَ بَلْ لَبِثْتَ مِائَةَ عامٍ فَانْظُرْ إِلى طَعامِكَ وَشَرابِكَ لَمْ يَتَسَنَّهْ وَانْظُرْ إِلى حِمارِكَ وَلِنَجْعَلَكَ آيَةً لِلنَّاسِ وَانْظُرْ إِلَى الْعِظامِ كَيْفَ نُنْشِزُها ثُمَّ نَكْسُوها لَحْماً فَلَمَّا تَبَيَّنَ لَهُ قالَ أَعْلَمُ أَنَّ اللَّهَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ (259)","quran_en":"259. Or the man who passed a town fallen in ruins upon its roofs. He said, “How will God give this life after its death?” God caused him to die for a hundred years, then raised him. He said, “How long have you remained?” He replied, “I have remained a day, or part of a day.” He said, “Rather, you have remained a hundred years. Look at your food and drink: they have not changed; and look at your donkey. We have done this to make you a sign for people. Look at the bones, how We raise them together and then clothe them with flesh.” When it became clear to him, he said, “I know that God has power over everything.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The comparison and the verb of change","html":"<p>I say: “Or” is a coordinating conjunction, and “like the one” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كالذي<\/bdi>) is coordinated with the relative expression governed by <i>ilā<\/i> in the preceding verse: have you not considered the man who disputed with Abraham concerning his Lord, and someone like the man who passed a town? The particle of comparison is introduced because those who deny resurrection are many, and those ignorant of its manner more numerous still; claimants to Lordship, by contrast, are few. Alternatively, the <i>kāf<\/i> is supplementary: have you not considered the man who disputed and the man who passed?<\/p><p><i>Annā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أنى<\/bdi>) is an adverb attached to “give life,” meaning “when,” or a circumstance meaning “how.” <i>Yatasannah<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يتسنه<\/bdi>) means change. Its original form is <i>yatasannan<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يتسنن<\/bdi>), the third <i>nūn<\/i> being replaced by a weak letter. The <i>Kāfiya<\/i> says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Replace the third of like consonants with a yāʾ,<br>as in “Khālid conjectured”—<i>taẓannā Khālidun taẓanniyā<\/i>.<\/blockquote><p>It thus becomes <i>tasannā<\/i>. The weak letter is then removed by the jussive, and the pause-<i>hāʾ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هاء السكت<\/bdi>) is added in both pause and connected recitation, as though compensating for the deleted letter. Another explanation derives it from <i>sana<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السنة<\/bdi>), conveying alteration with years; on this account the <i>hāʾ<\/i> is radical. “To make you” is coordinated with an omitted expression: so that you may take a lesson, and so that We may make you a sign for people.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-230\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B211]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Or” is a coordinating conjunction, and “like the one” ( كالذي ) is coordinated with the relative expression governed by ilā in the preceding verse: have you not considered the man who disputed with Abraham concerning his Lord, and someone like the man who passed a town? The particle of comparison is introduced because those who deny resurrection are many, and those ignorant of its manner more numerous still; claimants to Lordship, by contrast, are few. Alternatively, the kāf is supplementary: have you not considered the man who disputed and the man who passed? Annā ( أنى ) is an adverb attached to “give life,” meaning “when,” or a circumstance meaning “how.” Yatasannah ( يتسنه ) means change. Its original form is yatasannan ( يتسنن ), the third nūn being replaced by a weak letter. The Kāfiya says: Replace the third of like consonants with a yāʾ, as in “Khālid conjectured”— taẓannā Khālidun taẓanniyā . It thus becomes tasannā . The weak letter is then removed by the jussive, and the pause- hāʾ ( هاء السكت ) is added in both pause and connected recitation, as though compensating for the deleted letter. Another explanation derives it from sana ( السنة ), conveying alteration with years; on this account the hāʾ is radical. “To make you” is coordinated with an omitted expression: so that you may take a lesson, and so that We may make you a sign for people. [B211]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The food preserved and the donkey restored","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: have you not also considered, Muḥammad, someone like the man who passed a town? He was ʿUzayr, the learned man of Israel, though another person is also proposed. He passed Jerusalem after Nebuchadnezzar had laid it waste. Its walls had fallen upon its roofs: a hundred years had passed, the roofs had fallen and the walls collapsed upon them. Seeing it deserted and the bones of the dead decayed within it, he said to himself: “When will God bring this to life after its death?”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-231\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B212]<\/a><\/p><p>If the speaker was ʿUzayr, this acknowledged his inability to understand the manner of restoration and marvelled at the power of the Giver of life; if an unbeliever, it expressed doubt about its possibility. God kept him dead for a hundred years, then raised him to life. Through an angel, or without intermediary, He asked: “How long have you remained dead?” He replied: “A day, or part of a day.” He had died in the morning and was raised a hundred years later before sunset. Before looking at the sun, he said “a day”; turning and seeing some daylight remain, he corrected himself: “or part of a day.” The Real, glorious is His majesty, said: “Rather, you have remained a hundred years.”<\/p><p>ʿUzayr had gone to gather fruit for his family, placing a basket of grapes and a jar of juice upon his donkey. Passing the town, he tied the donkey and marvelled at its ruin and emptiness after its former habitation, saying what he said inwardly. God dealt gently with him and showed him directly how He gives life. He caused him to die for a hundred years, until the donkey’s bones decayed, while the juice and grapes remained as though freshly pressed and gathered.<\/p><p>He said: “Look at your food,” the grapes, “and your drink,” the juice: they have not changed through the passage of time and the long interval. Look at your donkey, whose joints have separated and bones decayed. We have done this so that you may witness Our power and become a sign for people after you. Look at the donkey’s bones: how We restore them to life, according to the reading <i>nunshiruhā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ننشرها<\/bdi>), from “God raised the dead”; or how We lift and assemble them, according to <i>nunshizuhā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ننشزها<\/bdi>), with <i>zāy<\/i>. Then We clothe them with flesh.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-232\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B211]<\/a><\/p><p>He looked at the bones, and each bone stood in its place. They were clothed with flesh and skin, and the donkey began to bray. When what had seemed strange and difficult became clear to him, he said: “I know with certain knowledge that God has power over everything.” Alternatively, when the truth—His power over all things—became clear, he said to himself: “Know that God has power over everything,” according to the imperative reading.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-233\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B211]<\/a><\/p><p>It is related that he returned to his people upon his donkey and said: “I am ʿUzayr.” They disbelieved him, so he recited the Torah from memory, which no one before him had memorised. They recognised him by this and said that he was God’s son—exalted is He above their saying. Another account says that he returned home as a young man and found his children elderly. Whenever he told them something, they said: “That is a story from a hundred years ago.” And God Most High knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-234\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B212]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: have you not also considered, Muḥammad, someone like the man who passed a town? He was ʿUzayr, the learned man of Israel, though another person is also proposed. He passed Jerusalem after Nebuchadnezzar had laid it waste. Its walls had fallen upon its roofs: a hundred years had passed, the roofs had fallen and the walls collapsed upon them. Seeing it deserted and the bones of the dead decayed within it, he said to himself: “When will God bring this to life after its death?” [B212] If the speaker was ʿUzayr, this acknowledged his inability to understand the manner of restoration and marvelled at the power of the Giver of life; if an unbeliever, it expressed doubt about its possibility. God kept him dead for a hundred years, then raised him to life. Through an angel, or without intermediary, He asked: “How long have you remained dead?” He replied: “A day, or part of a day.” He had died in the morning and was raised a hundred years later before sunset. Before looking at the sun, he said “a day”; turning and seeing some daylight remain, he corrected himself: “or part of a day.” The Real, glorious is His majesty, said: “Rather, you have remained a hundred years.” ʿUzayr had gone to gather fruit for his family, placing a basket of grapes and a jar of juice upon his donkey. Passing the town, he tied the donkey and marvelled at its ruin and emptiness after its former habitation, saying what he said inwardly. God dealt gently with him and showed him directly how He gives life. He caused him to die for a hundred years, until the donkey’s bones decayed, while the juice and grapes remained as though freshly pressed and gathered. He said: “Look at your food,” the grapes, “and your drink,” the juice: they have not changed through the passage of time and the long interval. Look at your donkey, whose joints have separated and bones decayed. We have done this so that you may witness Our power and become a sign for people after you. Look at the donkey’s bones: how We restore them to life, according to the reading nunshiruhā ( ننشرها ), from “God raised the dead”; or how We lift and assemble them, according to nunshizuhā ( ننشزها ), with zāy . Then We clothe them with flesh. [B211] He looked at the bones, and each bone stood in its place. They were clothed with flesh and skin, and the donkey began to bray. When what had seemed strange and difficult became clear to him, he said: “I know with certain knowledge that God has power over everything.” Alternatively, when the truth—His power over all things—became clear, he said to himself: “Know that God has power over everything,” according to the imperative reading. [B211] It is related that he returned to his people upon his donkey and said: “I am ʿUzayr.” They disbelieved him, so he recited the Torah from memory, which no one before him had memorised. They recognised him by this and said that he was God’s son—exalted is He above their saying. Another account says that he returned home as a young man and found his children elderly. Whenever he told them something, they said: “That is a story from a hundred years ago.” And God Most High knows best. [B212]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"From knowledge of certainty to its direct vision","html":"<p>This verse and the next indicate the command to cultivate certainty and ascend from knowledge of certainty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">علم اليقين<\/bdi>) to the eye of certainty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عين اليقين<\/bdi>). So long as the spirit remains veiled by stopping with causes and customary patterns, seeing sensory forms and remaining with intermediaries, it is not free of invading doubts and thoughts.<\/p><p>When it devotes itself entirely to its Lord and breaks through the customary habits of its self, the Real Most High uncovers for it the curtains of His unseen, reveals the hidden treasures of His mystery, unveils the secrets of the unseen kingdom and shows it the radiance of the world of divine omnipotence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجبروت<\/bdi>). It beholds the power of the Living who does not die and delights in witnessing the Essence and the lights of the attributes, in this life and after death. Doubts and imaginings then cease; it is purified of intrusive thoughts, and its illnesses and ailments disappear.<\/p><p>The <i>Ḥikam<\/i> says: “How should customary patterns be broken for you while you have not broken the customary habits of your self?” Consider ʿUzayr: the Real did not show him His power directly until He broke customary patterns for him, causing him to die and then bringing him to life. Likewise, disciple, do not hope that customary patterns will be broken for you, so that you witness the Real’s power or Essence directly, until you die to your interests and desire. Then your spirit and secret come to life; you witness your Lord’s mysteries and the curtains are lifted from the eye of your heart. And success is through God; He guides to the level path.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"This verse and the next indicate the command to cultivate certainty and ascend from knowledge of certainty ( علم اليقين ) to the eye of certainty ( عين اليقين ). So long as the spirit remains veiled by stopping with causes and customary patterns, seeing sensory forms and remaining with intermediaries, it is not free of invading doubts and thoughts. When it devotes itself entirely to its Lord and breaks through the customary habits of its self, the Real Most High uncovers for it the curtains of His unseen, reveals the hidden treasures of His mystery, unveils the secrets of the unseen kingdom and shows it the radiance of the world of divine omnipotence ( الجبروت ). It beholds the power of the Living who does not die and delights in witnessing the Essence and the lights of the attributes, in this life and after death. Doubts and imaginings then cease; it is purified of intrusive thoughts, and its illnesses and ailments disappear. The Ḥikam says: “How should customary patterns be broken for you while you have not broken the customary habits of your self?” Consider ʿUzayr: the Real did not show him His power directly until He broke customary patterns for him, causing him to die and then bringing him to life. Likewise, disciple, do not hope that customary patterns will be broken for you, so that you witness the Real’s power or Essence directly, until you die to your interests and desire. Then your spirit and secret come to life; you witness your Lord’s mysteries and the curtains are lifted from the eye of your heart. And success is through God; He guides to the level path."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then relates His intimate friend’s request to see power itself in the raising of the dead, so as to ascend from knowledge of certainty to its direct vision.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then relates His intimate friend’s request to see power itself in the raising of the dead, so as to ascend from knowledge of certainty to its direct vision."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C292.1","page":292,"ar":"خرف الرجل يخرفه: أخذ من طرف الفواكه. واختـرف: ذهب ليجتني التمر والفواكه.","en":"Kharafa, yakhrufuhu: a man took some fruit. Ikhtarafa: he went to gather dates and fruit.","anchor":"ليخترف"}],"english_characters":6325,"arabic_characters":4472},{"id":"q2-260","q":"2:260","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:260 · Abraham’s four birds and the four deaths of the self","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 293–295","cairo":"I:293–295","source_page_first":293,"source_page_last":295,"ar":"وَإِذْ قالَ إِبْراهِيمُ رَبِّ أَرِنِي كَيْفَ تُحْيِ الْمَوْتى قالَ أَوَلَمْ تُؤْمِنْ قالَ بَلى وَلكِنْ لِيَطْمَئِنَّ قَلْبِي قالَ فَخُذْ أَرْبَعَةً مِنَ الطَّيْرِ فَصُرْهُنَّ إِلَيْكَ ثُمَّ اجْعَلْ عَلى كُلِّ جَبَلٍ مِنْهُنَّ جُزْءاً ثُمَّ ادْعُهُنَّ يَأْتِينَكَ سَعْياً وَاعْلَمْ أَنَّ اللَّهَ عَزِيزٌ حَكِيمٌ (260)\nقلت: رأى: البصرية، إنما تتعدى إلى مفعول واحد، فإذا أُدخلت عليها الهمزة تعدت إلى مفعولين. وعلقها هنا عن الثاني الاستفهام، (وصرهن) أي: أَمْلهُنَّ واضمُمْهن إليك. وفيه لغتان: صار يصير ويصور، ولذلك قرئ بكسر الصاد وضمها، و (سعيا) : حال، أي: ساعيات.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكر يا محمد، أو أيها السامع، حين قالَ إِبْراهِيمُ عليه السلام: يا رَبِّ أَرِنِي كَيْفَ تُحْيِ الْمَوْتى أي: أبصرني كيفية إحياء الموتى، حتى أرى ذلك عياناً، أراد عليه السلام أن ينتقل من علم\nاليقين إلى عين اليقين، وقيل: لما قال للنمرود: رَبِّيَ الَّذِي يُحْيِي وَيُمِيتُ قال له: هل عاينت ذلك؟ فلم يقدر أن يقول: نعم. وانتقل إلى حجة أخرى، ثم سأل ربه أن يريه ذلك ليطمئن قلبه على الجواب، إن سئل مرة أخرى، فقال له الحق جل جلاله: أَوَلَمْ تُؤْمِنْ بأني قادر على الإحياء بإعادة التركيب والحياة؟ وإنما قال له ذلك، مع علمه بتحقيق إيمانه ليجيبه بما أجاب فيعلم السامعون غرضه، قالَ إبراهيم عليه السلام: بَلى آمنت إِنك على كل شيءٍ قدير، وَلكِنْ سألتك لِيَطْمَئِنَّ قَلْبِي إذ ليس الخبر كالعيان، وليس علم اليقين كعين اليقين، أراد أن يضم الشهود والعيان إلى الوحي والبرهان.\nقال له الحق جلّ جلاله: فَخُذْ أَرْبَعَةً مِنَ الطَّيْرِ طاووساً وديكاً وغراباً وحمامة، ومنهم من ذكر النسر بدل الحمام، فَصُرْهُنَّ إِلَيْكَ أي: اضممهن إليك لتتأملها وتعرف أشكالها، لئلا يلتبس عليك بعد الإحياء أشكالها، ثُمَّ اجْعَلْ عَلى كُلِّ جَبَلٍ مِنْهُنَّ جُزْءاً أي: ثم جَزَّئهن، وفرق أجزاءهن على الجبال التي تحضرك. قيل: كانت أربعة وقيل: سبعة، ثُمَّ ادْعُهُنَّ وقل لهن: تعالين بإذن الله، يَأْتِينَكَ سَعْياً أي: ساعيات مسرعات، رُوِيَ أنه أمر أن يذبحها وينتف ريشها، ويقطعها ويخلط بعضها ببعض، ويوزعها على الجبال، ويمسك رءوسها عنده، ثم يناديها، ففعل ذلك، فجَعَل كل جزء يطير إلى الآخر ويلتئم بصاحبه حتى صارت جثثاً، ثم أقبل إليه فأعطى كل طير رأسه فطار في الهواء. فسبحان من لا يعجزه شيء، ولا يغيب عن علمه شيء، ثم نبّه إلى التفكر في عجائب قدرته وحكمته فقال: وَاعْلَمْ أَنَّ اللَّهَ عَزِيزٌ لا يعجزه شيء، حَكِيمٌ ذو حكمة بالغة فيما يفعل ويذر.\nالإشارة: من أراد أن تحيا رُوحُه الحياة الأبدية، وينتقل من علم اليقين إلى عين اليقين، فلا بد أن تموت نفسه أربع موتات:\nالأولى: تموت عن حب الشهوات والزخارف الدنيوية، التي هي صفة الطاووس.\nالثانية: عن الصولة والقوى النفسانية، التي هي صفة الديك.\nالثالثة: عن خسة النفس والدناءة وبعد الأمل، التي هي صفة الغراب.\nالرابعة: عن الترفع والمسارعة إلى الهوى المتصف بها الحمام.\nفإذا ذبح نفسه عن هذه الخصال حييتْ روحه، وتهذبت نفسه، فصارت طوع يده، كلما دعاها إلى طاعة أتت إليها مسرعة ساعية.\nوإلى هذا المعنى أشار الشيخ أبو الحسن الشاذلي بقوله في حزبه الكبير: (واجعل لنا ظهيراً من عقولنا ومهيمناً من أرواحنا، ومسخراً من أنفسنا، كي نسبحك كثيراً، ونذكرك كثيرًا، إِنكَ كنت بنا بصيراً) .\nولما كانت حياة الروح متوقفة على أمرين: بذل النفوس، ودفع الفلوس وقدم الإشارة إلى الأول بقوله: وَقاتِلُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ، أشار إلى الثاني بقوله:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ قالَ إِبْراهِيمُ رَبِّ أَرِنِي كَيْفَ تُحْيِ الْمَوْتى قالَ أَوَلَمْ تُؤْمِنْ قالَ بَلى وَلكِنْ لِيَطْمَئِنَّ قَلْبِي قالَ فَخُذْ أَرْبَعَةً مِنَ الطَّيْرِ فَصُرْهُنَّ إِلَيْكَ ثُمَّ اجْعَلْ عَلى كُلِّ جَبَلٍ مِنْهُنَّ جُزْءاً ثُمَّ ادْعُهُنَّ يَأْتِينَكَ سَعْياً وَاعْلَمْ أَنَّ اللَّهَ عَزِيزٌ حَكِيمٌ (260)","quran_en":"260. When Abraham said, “My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead,” He said, “Do you not believe?” He replied, “Yes indeed, but so that my heart may be at rest.” He said, “Then take four birds and draw them to you. Place a portion of them upon each mountain, then call them: they will come to you swiftly. Know that God is Mighty, Wise.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Seeing, showing and drawing near","html":"<p>I say: <i>raʾā<\/i>, when it denotes visual seeing, governs one object. Introducing the causative <i>hamza<\/i> makes it govern two. Here the interrogative suspends its direct government of the second object (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التعليق بالاستفهام<\/bdi>). “Draw them to you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صرهن إليك<\/bdi>) means incline and gather them towards yourself. There are two verbal forms, <i>ṣāra yaṣīru<\/i> and <i>ṣāra yaṣūru<\/i>, hence the readings with <i>kasra<\/i> and <i>ḍamma<\/i> on the <i>ṣād<\/i>. “Swiftly” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سعياً<\/bdi>) is a circumstantial expression: coming swiftly.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: raʾā , when it denotes visual seeing, governs one object. Introducing the causative hamza makes it govern two. Here the interrogative suspends its direct government of the second object ( التعليق بالاستفهام ). “Draw them to you” ( صرهن إليك ) means incline and gather them towards yourself. There are two verbal forms, ṣāra yaṣīru and ṣāra yaṣūru , hence the readings with kasra and ḍamma on the ṣād . “Swiftly” ( سعياً ) is a circumstantial expression: coming swiftly."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Adding direct witnessing to revelation and proof","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember, Muḥammad—or listener—when Abraham said: “My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead.” Let me see its manner directly. He wished to pass from knowledge<\/p><p>of certainty to the eye of certainty. Another account says that when he told Nimrod, “My Lord is He who gives life and causes death,” Nimrod asked whether he had witnessed it. Unable to say yes, he moved to another proof. He then asked his Lord to show it to him, so that his heart would be at rest concerning his answer if questioned again.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-235\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B213]<\/a><\/p><p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, asked: “Do you not believe that I can give life by restoring the composition and life?” Although He knew the reality of Abraham’s faith, He asked so that Abraham would give the answer he gave, making his purpose known to the listeners. Abraham replied: “Yes indeed, I believe that You have power over everything. I ask only that my heart may be at rest.” A report is not like direct vision, nor is knowledge of certainty like the eye of certainty. He wished to add witnessing and direct vision to revelation and demonstration (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الوحي والبرهان<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>The Real said: “Take four birds”: a peacock, a cock, a raven and a pigeon; some name a vulture instead of the pigeon. Draw them to you, examine them and know their forms, so that their forms will not be confused for you after restoration. Then divide them and place portions upon the mountains near you—said to have been four, or seven. Call them, saying, “Come by God’s leave,” and they will come swiftly.<\/p><p>It is related that he was commanded to slaughter them, pluck their feathers, cut them up, mix their parts, distribute them upon the mountains and retain their heads. Then he was to call them. He did so. Each part flew to another and joined its counterpart until bodies were formed. They approached him, and he gave each bird its head; it flew into the air. Glory to Him whom nothing defeats and from whose knowledge nothing is absent. He then directs attention to reflection upon the wonders of His power and wisdom: “Know that God is Mighty”—nothing defeats Him—“Wise,” possessed of consummate wisdom in what He does and leaves undone.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember, Muḥammad—or listener—when Abraham said: “My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead.” Let me see its manner directly. He wished to pass from knowledge of certainty to the eye of certainty. Another account says that when he told Nimrod, “My Lord is He who gives life and causes death,” Nimrod asked whether he had witnessed it. Unable to say yes, he moved to another proof. He then asked his Lord to show it to him, so that his heart would be at rest concerning his answer if questioned again. [B213] The Real, glorious is His majesty, asked: “Do you not believe that I can give life by restoring the composition and life?” Although He knew the reality of Abraham’s faith, He asked so that Abraham would give the answer he gave, making his purpose known to the listeners. Abraham replied: “Yes indeed, I believe that You have power over everything. I ask only that my heart may be at rest.” A report is not like direct vision, nor is knowledge of certainty like the eye of certainty. He wished to add witnessing and direct vision to revelation and demonstration ( الوحي والبرهان ). The Real said: “Take four birds”: a peacock, a cock, a raven and a pigeon; some name a vulture instead of the pigeon. Draw them to you, examine them and know their forms, so that their forms will not be confused for you after restoration. Then divide them and place portions upon the mountains near you—said to have been four, or seven. Call them, saying, “Come by God’s leave,” and they will come swiftly. It is related that he was commanded to slaughter them, pluck their feathers, cut them up, mix their parts, distribute them upon the mountains and retain their heads. Then he was to call them. He did so. Each part flew to another and joined its counterpart until bodies were formed. They approached him, and he gave each bird its head; it flew into the air. Glory to Him whom nothing defeats and from whose knowledge nothing is absent. He then directs attention to reflection upon the wonders of His power and wisdom: “Know that God is Mighty”—nothing defeats Him—“Wise,” possessed of consummate wisdom in what He does and leaves undone."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Four qualities to be overcome","html":"<p>Whoever wishes his spirit to live the everlasting life and to pass from knowledge of certainty to the eye of certainty must have his self die four deaths.<\/p><p>First, it must die to love of appetites and worldly adornments: the peacock’s quality. Second, to aggression and the powers of the lower self: the cock’s quality. Third, to baseness, lowliness of character and far-reaching hopes: the raven’s quality. Fourth, to self-exaltation and hastening towards desire: the pigeon’s quality.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-236\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B213]<\/a><\/p><p>When he slaughters these qualities in his self, his spirit lives and his self becomes refined and obedient to his hand. Whenever he calls it to obedience, it comes swiftly and eagerly. Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan al-Shādhilī indicates this meaning in his Great Litany (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحزب الكبير<\/bdi>): “Make our intellects a support for us, our spirits a guardian over us, and our selves subject to us, so that we may glorify You much and remember You much. You have ever seen us.”<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever wishes his spirit to live the everlasting life and to pass from knowledge of certainty to the eye of certainty must have his self die four deaths. First, it must die to love of appetites and worldly adornments: the peacock’s quality. Second, to aggression and the powers of the lower self: the cock’s quality. Third, to baseness, lowliness of character and far-reaching hopes: the raven’s quality. Fourth, to self-exaltation and hastening towards desire: the pigeon’s quality. [B213] When he slaughters these qualities in his self, his spirit lives and his self becomes refined and obedient to his hand. Whenever he calls it to obedience, it comes swiftly and eagerly. Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan al-Shādhilī indicates this meaning in his Great Litany ( الحزب الكبير ): “Make our intellects a support for us, our spirits a guardian over us, and our selves subject to us, so that we may glorify You much and remember You much. You have ever seen us.”"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The spirit’s life depends upon two things: offering up the self and giving away money. Having indicated the first through “Fight in the path of God,” He now indicates the second.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The spirit’s life depends upon two things: offering up the self and giving away money. Having indicated the first through “Fight in the path of God,” He now indicates the second."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3782,"arabic_characters":2987},{"id":"q2-261-262","q":"2:261–262","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:261–262 · The seven hundred grains and giving without injury","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 295–296","cairo":"I:295–296","source_page_first":295,"source_page_last":296,"ar":"مَثَلُ الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ كَمَثَلِ حَبَّةٍ أَنْبَتَتْ سَبْعَ سَنابِلَ فِي كُلِّ سُنْبُلَةٍ مِائَةُ حَبَّةٍ وَاللَّهُ يُضاعِفُ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ واسِعٌ عَلِيمٌ (261) الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ ثُمَّ لا يُتْبِعُونَ ما أَنْفَقُوا مَنًّا وَلا أَذىً لَهُمْ أَجْرُهُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِمْ وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ (262)\nقلت: (مثل الذين) : مبتدأ، و (كمثل) : خبر، ولا بد من حذف مضاف، إما من المبتدأ أو الخبر، أي: مثل نفقة الذين ينفقون كمثل حبة، أو مثل الذين ينفقون كمثل باذر حبة ... الخ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في التحريض على النفقة في سبيل الله: مَثَلُ الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ أي: يتصدقون بها في سبيل الله، كالجهاد ونحوه، كَمَثَلِ زارع حَبَّةٍ أَنْبَتَتْ له سَبْعَ سَنابِلَ فِي كُلِّ سُنْبُلَةٍ مِائَةُ حَبَّةٍ، فالمجموع سبعمائة. وفى الحديث عنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «الحسنة بعشر أمثالها، إلى سبعمائة إلى أضْعَافٍ كَثيرَةٍ» . وإسناد الإنبات إلى الحبة مجاز، والمنبت هو الله، وهذا مثال لا يقتضي الوقوع، وقد يقع في الذرة والدخن(1) في الأرض الطيبة، بحيث تخرج الحبة ساقاً يتشعب إلى سبع شعب، في كل شعبة سنبلة، وَاللَّهُ يُضاعِفُ تلك المضاعفة لِمَنْ يَشاءُ بفضله، على حسب حال المنفق من إخلاصه وتعبه، وبحسبه تتفاوت الأعمال في مقادير الثواب، وَاللَّهُ واسِعٌ لا يضيق عليه ما يتفضل به من الثواب، عَلِيمٌ بنية المنفق وقدر إنفاقه.\nثم ذكر شرطَيْن آخرَيْن في قبول النفقة، فقال: الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ ثُمَّ لا يُتْبِعُونَ ما أَنْفَقُوا مَنًّا وَلا أَذىً. المن: أن يعتد بإحسانه على من أحسن إليه بحيث يقول: أنا فعلت معه كذا، وكذا إظهاراً لميزته عليه. والأذى: أن يتطاول عليه بذلك. ويقول: لولا أنا لم يكن منك شيء، مثلاً. فمن فعل هذا فقد ذهبت صدقته هباءاً منثوراً، ومن سلم من ذلك، وأنفق ماله ابتغاء وجه الله ف- لا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ.\nوقال زيد بن أسلم رضي الله عنه: إذا أعطيت أحداً شيئاً وظننت أن سلامَكَ يَثْقُلُ عليه فَكُفَ سلامَكَ عنه. هـ.\nقيل: إن الآية نزلت في عثمان وعبد الرحمن بن عوف- رضى الله عنهما أما عثمان فإنه جهز جيش العسرة بألف بعير بأقتابها وأحلاسها. وقال عبد الرحمن بن سمرة: جاء عُثْمَانُ بألْفِ دِينَارِ في جيش العُسْرَةِ، فصَبَها في حجر النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، فرأيت النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم يُدخل يَده فيها، ويُقلِّبهَا ويقول: «ما ضَرَّ ابن عفَّان ما عَمِلَ بَعْدَ اليوم» .\nزاد في رواية أبى سعيد: فرأيت النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم رافعاً يدعو لعثمان، ويقول: «يا رب عثمان بن عفان، رضيتُ عنه فارض عنه» . وأما عبد الرحمن: فإنه أتى النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم بأربعة آلاف درهم، صدقة، وأمسك أربعة آلاف لعياله، فقال له النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «بارك الله لك فيما أعطيتَ وفيما أمسكتَ» .\nوإنما لم يدخل الفاء في قوله: لا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ، مع أن الموصول قد تضمن معنى الشرط، إيهاماً بأنهم أهلٌ لذلك، وإن لم يفعلوا، فكيف بهم إذا فعلوا. قاله البيضاوي.\nالإشارة: التقرُّب إلى الله تعالى يكون بالعمل البدني وبالعمل المالي، وبالعمل القلبي، أما العمل البدني، ويدخل فيه العمل اللساني، فقد ورد فيه التضعيف بعشر وبعشرين وبثلاثين وبخمسين وبمائة، وبأكثر من ذلك أو أقل، وكذلك العمل المالي: قد ورد تضعيفه إلى سبع مائة، ويتفاوت ذلك بحسب النيات والمقاصد، وأما العمل القلبي: فليس له أجر محصور، قال تعالى: إِنَّما يُوَفَّى الصَّابِرُونَ أَجْرَهُمْ بِغَيْرِ حِسابٍ، فالصبر، والخوف، والرجاء، والورع، والزهد، والتوكل، والمحبة، والرضا، والتسليم، والمعرفة، وحسن الخلق، والفكرة، وسائر الأخلاق الحميدة، إنما جزاؤها: الرضا، والإقبال والتقريب، وحسن الوصال. قال تعالى: وَرِضْوانٌ مِنَ اللَّهِ أَكْبَرُ أي:\nأكبر من الجزاء الحسيّ الذي هو القصور والحور.\nوأما قوله عليه الصلاة والسلام: «تفكر ساعة أفضل من عبادة سبعين سنة» . فإنما هو كناية عن الكثرة والمبالغة، كقوله تعالى: إِنْ تَسْتَغْفِرْ لَهُمْ سَبْعِينَ مَرَّةً فَلَنْ يَغْفِرَ اللَّهُ لَهُمْ. ومثله قول الشاعر(1) :\nكُلُّ وَقتٍ مِنْ حَبيبيِ ... قَدْرُهُ كألفِ حجَّهْ\nأي: سَنَة. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم بيَّن الحقّ تعالى أن حسن الخلق ولين الجانب أفضل من الصدقة المشوبة، فقال:","quran_ar":"مَثَلُ الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ كَمَثَلِ حَبَّةٍ أَنْبَتَتْ سَبْعَ سَنابِلَ فِي كُلِّ سُنْبُلَةٍ مِائَةُ حَبَّةٍ وَاللَّهُ يُضاعِفُ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ وَاللَّهُ واسِعٌ عَلِيمٌ (261) الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ ثُمَّ لا يُتْبِعُونَ ما أَنْفَقُوا مَنًّا وَلا أَذىً لَهُمْ أَجْرُهُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِمْ وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ (262)","quran_en":"261. The likeness of those who spend their wealth in the path of God is a grain that grows seven ears, in every ear a hundred grains. God multiplies for whom He wills. God is All-Encompassing, Knowing. 262. Those who spend their wealth in the path of God, then do not follow what they spend with reminders of generosity or injury, have their reward with their Lord. No fear shall be upon them, nor shall they grieve.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The implied term in the comparison","html":"<p>I say: “The likeness of those” is the subject, and “like a grain” the predicate. A term in a possessive construction must be supplied on one side or the other: the likeness of the expenditure of those who spend is like a grain; or the likeness of those who spend is like someone sowing a grain—and so forth.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “The likeness of those” is the subject, and “like a grain” the predicate. A term in a possessive construction must be supplied on one side or the other: the likeness of the expenditure of those who spend is like a grain; or the likeness of those who spend is like someone sowing a grain—and so forth."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The multiplication of sincere charity","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, encourages expenditure in His path: the likeness of those who spend their wealth in His path, giving charity for such purposes as fighting in His cause, is a sower whose grain produces seven ears, with a hundred grains in each: seven hundred altogether. The Prophet said: “A good deed is multiplied tenfold, up to seven hundredfold, and many times beyond.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-237\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B214]<\/a><\/p><p>Ascribing the growth to the grain is figurative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مجاز<\/bdi>); the One who causes growth is God. This is an illustration, not a claim that such growth must actually occur, though it may occur with sorghum or millet in good soil: a grain produces a stalk branching into seven, each branch bearing an ear. God grants this multiplication through His bounty to whom He wills, according to the giver’s sincerity and effort. Accordingly, deeds differ in the amount of their recompense. God is All-Encompassing: what He bestows of recompense does not restrict Him. He knows the giver’s intention and the measure of his giving.<\/p><p>He then mentions two further conditions for accepted expenditure: those who spend in God’s path must not follow it with reminders of their favour or injury (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">منّ وأذى<\/bdi>). Reminding someone of a favour means counting one’s beneficence against its recipient, saying, “I did such-and-such for him,” to display one’s superiority. Injury means lording it over him because of it, saying, for example: “Without me, you would have amounted to nothing.” Whoever does this has made his charity scattered dust. Those free from it, who spend seeking God’s Face, shall have no fear and shall not grieve.<\/p><p>Zayd ibn Aslam said: “If you give someone something and think that your greeting weighs upon him, withhold even your greeting from him.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, encourages expenditure in His path: the likeness of those who spend their wealth in His path, giving charity for such purposes as fighting in His cause, is a sower whose grain produces seven ears, with a hundred grains in each: seven hundred altogether. The Prophet said: “A good deed is multiplied tenfold, up to seven hundredfold, and many times beyond.” [B214] Ascribing the growth to the grain is figurative ( مجاز ); the One who causes growth is God. This is an illustration, not a claim that such growth must actually occur, though it may occur with sorghum or millet in good soil: a grain produces a stalk branching into seven, each branch bearing an ear. God grants this multiplication through His bounty to whom He wills, according to the giver’s sincerity and effort. Accordingly, deeds differ in the amount of their recompense. God is All-Encompassing: what He bestows of recompense does not restrict Him. He knows the giver’s intention and the measure of his giving. He then mentions two further conditions for accepted expenditure: those who spend in God’s path must not follow it with reminders of their favour or injury ( منّ وأذى ). Reminding someone of a favour means counting one’s beneficence against its recipient, saying, “I did such-and-such for him,” to display one’s superiority. Injury means lording it over him because of it, saying, for example: “Without me, you would have amounted to nothing.” Whoever does this has made his charity scattered dust. Those free from it, who spend seeking God’s Face, shall have no fear and shall not grieve. Zayd ibn Aslam said: “If you give someone something and think that your greeting weighs upon him, withhold even your greeting from him.”"},{"type":"language","title":"Generosity and the promised reward","html":"<p>The verse is said to have been revealed concerning ʿUthmān and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf. ʿUthmān equipped the Army of Hardship with a thousand camels, complete with saddles and saddlecloths. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samura said: “ʿUthmān brought a thousand dinars for the Army of Hardship and poured them into the Prophet’s lap. I saw him put his hand among them and turn them over, saying: ‘Nothing Ibn ʿAffān does after today will harm him.’” Abū Saʿīd’s transmission adds: “I saw the Prophet raise his hands in prayer for ʿUthmān, saying: ‘Lord of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, I am pleased with him; be pleased with him.’”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-238\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B214]<\/a><\/p><p>ʿAbd al-Raḥmān brought four thousand dirhams to the Prophet as charity and kept four thousand for his dependants. The Prophet said: “May God bless what you have given and what you have retained.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-239\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B214]<\/a><\/p><p>Although the relative expression contains a conditional meaning, the <i>fāʾ<\/i> is not introduced before “No fear shall be upon them.” This suggests that they deserve it even had they not acted thus—how much more when they do. Al-Bayḍāwī says this.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The verse is said to have been revealed concerning ʿUthmān and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf. ʿUthmān equipped the Army of Hardship with a thousand camels, complete with saddles and saddlecloths. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samura said: “ʿUthmān brought a thousand dinars for the Army of Hardship and poured them into the Prophet’s lap. I saw him put his hand among them and turn them over, saying: ‘Nothing Ibn ʿAffān does after today will harm him.’” Abū Saʿīd’s transmission adds: “I saw the Prophet raise his hands in prayer for ʿUthmān, saying: ‘Lord of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, I am pleased with him; be pleased with him.’” [B214] ʿAbd al-Raḥmān brought four thousand dirhams to the Prophet as charity and kept four thousand for his dependants. The Prophet said: “May God bless what you have given and what you have retained.” [B214] Although the relative expression contains a conditional meaning, the fāʾ is not introduced before “No fear shall be upon them.” This suggests that they deserve it even had they not acted thus—how much more when they do. Al-Bayḍāwī says this."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The unbounded recompense of inward acts","html":"<p>Drawing near to God Most High occurs through bodily, financial and inward acts. Bodily acts include acts of the tongue. Their multiplication is reported as ten, twenty, thirty, fifty, a hundred, more or less. Financial acts are reported to be multiplied up to seven hundredfold. This varies according to intentions and purposes.<\/p><p>Inward acts, however, have no circumscribed reward: “The steadfast are paid their reward without reckoning” [39:10]. Patience, fear, hope, scrupulousness, renunciation, reliance, love, contentment, surrender, gnosis, good character, contemplation and the other praiseworthy qualities receive divine good pleasure, turning towards the servant, drawing him near and beautiful communion. “God’s good pleasure is greater” [9:72]: greater than sensory recompense, such as palaces and heavenly companions.<\/p><p>The saying attributed to the Prophet, “An hour’s contemplation is better than seventy years’ worship,” expresses abundance and intensification, as in “Even if you seek forgiveness for them seventy times, God will not forgive them” [9:80]. The poet’s words are similar:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Every moment from my Beloved<br>is worth a thousand ḥijjas—<\/blockquote><p>that is, years. And God Most High knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-240\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B215]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Drawing near to God Most High occurs through bodily, financial and inward acts. Bodily acts include acts of the tongue. Their multiplication is reported as ten, twenty, thirty, fifty, a hundred, more or less. Financial acts are reported to be multiplied up to seven hundredfold. This varies according to intentions and purposes. Inward acts, however, have no circumscribed reward: “The steadfast are paid their reward without reckoning” [39:10]. Patience, fear, hope, scrupulousness, renunciation, reliance, love, contentment, surrender, gnosis, good character, contemplation and the other praiseworthy qualities receive divine good pleasure, turning towards the servant, drawing him near and beautiful communion. “God’s good pleasure is greater” [9:72]: greater than sensory recompense, such as palaces and heavenly companions. The saying attributed to the Prophet, “An hour’s contemplation is better than seventy years’ worship,” expresses abundance and intensification, as in “Even if you seek forgiveness for them seventy times, God will not forgive them” [9:80]. The poet’s words are similar: Every moment from my Beloved is worth a thousand ḥijjas— that is, years. And God Most High knows best. [B215]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then explains that good character and gentleness are better than charity mixed with impurity.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then explains that good character and gentleness are better than charity mixed with impurity."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C295.1","page":295,"ar":"الدخن: نبات عشبي من النجيليات، حبه صغير أملس، كحب السمسم، ينبت بريا ومزروعا.","en":"Millet is an herbaceous plant of the grass family. Its grain is small and smooth, like sesame seed; it grows both wild and cultivated.","anchor":"الدخن"},{"id":"C296.1","page":296,"ar":"وهو الششتري.","en":"He is al-Shushtarī.","anchor":"ومثله قول الشاعر"}],"english_characters":4417,"arabic_characters":3738},{"id":"q2-263","q":"2:263","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:263 · A gracious word and forgiveness","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 296–297","cairo":"I:296–297","source_page_first":296,"source_page_last":297,"ar":"قَوْلٌ مَعْرُوفٌ وَمَغْفِرَةٌ خَيْرٌ مِنْ صَدَقَةٍ يَتْبَعُها أَذىً وَاللَّهُ غَنِيٌّ حَلِيمٌ (263)\nقلت: (قول) : مبتدأ، و (خير) : خبر، والمسوِّغ الصفةُ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: قَوْلٌ جميل يقوله الإنسان للسائل في حال رده، حيث لم يجد ما يعطيه، خَيْرٌ وأفضل عند الله من الصدقة التي يتبعها المن والأذى، ومثال القول المعروف: الله يرزقنا وإياك رزقاً حسناً. والله يغنينا وإياك من فضله العظيم، وشبه ذلك من غير تعبيس ولا كراهية. وَمَغْفِرَةٌ للسائل والعفو عن جفوته وإلحاحه، خَيْرٌ أيضاً مِنْ صَدَقَةٍ يَتْبَعُها مَنٍّ، أو أَذىً للسائل، علم الحق جل جلاله أن الفقير إذا رُدَّ بغير نوال شقَّ عليه، فربما أطلق لسانه وأظهر الشكوى فأمر المسئول بالعفو والتواضع. ولو شاء الحقّ تعالى لأغنى الجميع، لكنه أعطى الأغنياء ليظهر شكرهم، وابتلى الفقراء لينظر كيف صبرهم، وَاللَّهُ تعالى غَنِيٌّ عن إنفاق يصحبه مَنٌ أو أذى، حَلِيمٌ عن معاجلة من يَمُنُّ أو يؤذي بالعقوبة. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: يُفهم من الآية أن حسن الخلق، ولين الجانب، وخفض الجناح، وكف الأذى، وحمل الجفاء، وشهود الصفاء، من أفضل الأعمال وأزكى الأحوال وأحسن الخلال، وفي الحديث: «إنَّ حُسْن الخُلق يعدل الصيام والقيام» .\nوفي قوله: وَاللَّهُ غَنِيٌّ حَلِيمٌ: تربية للسائل والمسئول، فتربية السائل: أن يستغني بالغنيِّ الكبير عن سؤال العبد الفقير، ويكتفي بعلم الحال عن المقال، وتربية المسئول: أن يحلم عن جفوة السائل فيتلطف في الخطاب، ويحسن الرد والجواب. قال في شرح الأسماء: والتخلق بهذا الاسم- يعني الحليم- بالصفح عن الجنايات، والسمح فيما يقابلونه به من الإساءات، بل يجازيهم بالإحسان، تحقيقاً للحلم والغفران. هـ.\nثم حذَّر الحق تعالى من المن والأذى فى الصدقة، فقال:","quran_ar":"قَوْلٌ مَعْرُوفٌ وَمَغْفِرَةٌ خَيْرٌ مِنْ صَدَقَةٍ يَتْبَعُها أَذىً وَاللَّهُ غَنِيٌّ حَلِيمٌ (263)","quran_en":"263. A gracious word and forgiveness are better than charity followed by injury. God is Independent, Forbearing.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"An indefinite subject qualified by an adjective","html":"<p>I say: “A word” is the subject and “better” the predicate. The adjective makes the indefinite subject admissible.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “A word” is the subject and “better” the predicate. The adjective makes the indefinite subject admissible."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Gentleness towards the person who asks","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: a gracious word spoken to someone asking, when one has nothing to give and must turn him away, is better before God than charity followed by reminders and injury. Such words include: “May God provide us and you with a good provision”; “May God enrich us and you from His immense bounty”—and the like, without frowning or aversion.<\/p><p>Forgiving the person who asks, overlooking his roughness and insistence, is also better than charity followed by reminders or injury. The Real knows that being sent away empty-handed is hard for the poor person. He may therefore loose his tongue and complain. The person asked is consequently commanded to pardon and show humility.<\/p><p>Had the Real willed, He would have enriched everyone. He gives to the rich so that their gratitude may appear, and tests the poor to see how they endure. God is independent of expenditure accompanied by reminders or injury, and Forbearing in not hastening to punish those who remind or injure. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: a gracious word spoken to someone asking, when one has nothing to give and must turn him away, is better before God than charity followed by reminders and injury. Such words include: “May God provide us and you with a good provision”; “May God enrich us and you from His immense bounty”—and the like, without frowning or aversion. Forgiving the person who asks, overlooking his roughness and insistence, is also better than charity followed by reminders or injury. The Real knows that being sent away empty-handed is hard for the poor person. He may therefore loose his tongue and complain. The person asked is consequently commanded to pardon and show humility. Had the Real willed, He would have enriched everyone. He gives to the rich so that their gratitude may appear, and tests the poor to see how they endure. God is independent of expenditure accompanied by reminders or injury, and Forbearing in not hastening to punish those who remind or injure. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The divine Names educate giver and petitioner","html":"<p>The verse teaches that good character, gentleness, humility, refraining from harm, bearing rough treatment and witnessing purity are among the best deeds, purest states and finest qualities. The hadith says: “Good character equals fasting and standing in prayer.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-241\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B216]<\/a><\/p><p>“God is Independent, Forbearing” educates both the petitioner and the person asked. The petitioner is taught to find sufficiency in the Great, Independent One rather than ask a needy servant, and to be content with His knowledge of the situation instead of speaking. The person asked is taught to bear the petitioner’s roughness patiently, address him gently and make a gracious response.<\/p><p>The commentary on the divine Names says: taking on the appropriate character of this Name—the Forbearing—means overlooking offences and being generous towards the wrongs people commit against one. Rather, one repays them with goodness, realizing forbearance and forgiveness.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-242\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B216]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The verse teaches that good character, gentleness, humility, refraining from harm, bearing rough treatment and witnessing purity are among the best deeds, purest states and finest qualities. The hadith says: “Good character equals fasting and standing in prayer.” [B216] “God is Independent, Forbearing” educates both the petitioner and the person asked. The petitioner is taught to find sufficiency in the Great, Independent One rather than ask a needy servant, and to be content with His knowledge of the situation instead of speaking. The person asked is taught to bear the petitioner’s roughness patiently, address him gently and make a gracious response. The commentary on the divine Names says: taking on the appropriate character of this Name—the Forbearing—means overlooking offences and being generous towards the wrongs people commit against one. Rather, one repays them with goodness, realizing forbearance and forgiveness. [B216]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then warns against reminders and injury in charity.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then warns against reminders and injury in charity."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2141,"arabic_characters":1498},{"id":"q2-264","q":"2:264","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:264 · The smooth rock and charity performed for display","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 297–298","cairo":"I:297–298","source_page_first":297,"source_page_last":298,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تُبْطِلُوا صَدَقاتِكُمْ بِالْمَنِّ وَالْأَذى كَالَّذِي يُنْفِقُ مالَهُ رِئاءَ النَّاسِ وَلا يُؤْمِنُ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ فَمَثَلُهُ كَمَثَلِ صَفْوانٍ عَلَيْهِ تُرابٌ فَأَصابَهُ وابِلٌ فَتَرَكَهُ صَلْداً لا يَقْدِرُونَ عَلى شَيْءٍ مِمَّا كَسَبُوا وَاللَّهُ لا يَهْدِي الْقَوْمَ الْكافِرِينَ (264)\nقلت: (كالذي) : الكاف في محل نصب على المصدر، أي: إبطالاً كإبطال الذي ينفق ماله رئاء الناس. أو حال، أي: مشبَّهين بالذي ينفق رئاء. و (رئاء) مفعول له، والصفوان: الحجر الأملس، والصلد: البارز الذي لا تراب عليه، وجمع الضمير في قوله: (لا يقدرون) باعتبار معنى (الذي) لأنَّ المراد به الجنس.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تُبْطِلُوا أجر صدقتكم بسبب الْمَنِّ بها على المتصدَّقِ عليه، وَالْأَذى الذي يصدر منكم له، بأن تذكروا ذلك للناس، فتكون صدقتكم باطلة، كَالَّذِي يُنْفِقُ مالَهُ رِئاءَ النَّاسِ وَلا يُؤْمِنُ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ، فإن أجره يوم القيامة يكون هباء منثوراً، فَمَثَلُهُ في انتفاعه بصدقته، وتستره بها في دار الدنيا، وافتضاحه يوم القيامة، كحجر أملس عَلَيْهِ تُرابٌ يستره، فيظن الرائي أنه أرض طيبة تصلح للزراعة، فَأَصابَهُ وابِلٌ أي: مطر غزير فَتَرَكَهُ صَلْداً حجراً يابساً خالياً من التراب، كذلك المراءون بأعمالهم، ينتفعون بها في الدنيا بثناء الناس عليهم وستر حالهم، فإذا قدموا يوم القيامة وجدوها باطلة، لا يَقْدِرُونَ عَلى الانتفاع ب شَيْءٍ مِمَّا كَسَبُوا وَاللَّهُ لا يَهْدِي الْقَوْمَ الْكافِرِينَ إلى مراشدهم ومصالح دينهم. وفيه تعريض بأن الرياء والمن والأذى من صفة الكافر، ولا بد للمؤمن أن يتجنب عنها.\nوبالله التوفيق.\nالإشارة: تصفية الأعمال على قدر تصفية القلوب، وتصفية القلوب على قدر مراقبة علام الغيوب، والمراقبة على قدر المعرفة. والمعرفة على قدر المشاهدة. والمشاهدة تحصل على قدر المجاهدة، وَالَّذِينَ جاهَدُوا فِينا لَنَهْدِيَنَّهُمْ سُبُلَنا. وفي الحكم: «حسن الأعمال من نتائج حسن الأحوال. وحسن الأحوال من التحقق بمقامات الإنزال» والحاصل أن من لم يتحقق بمقام الفناء لا تخلو أعماله من شوب الخلل، ومن تحقق بالزوال لم ير لنفسه نسبة في عطاء ولا منع، ولا حركة ولا سكون، ولم ير لغيره وجوداً حتى يرجو منه نفعاً ولا خيراً. وفي بعض الإشارات: يا من يرائي أَمْر مَنْ ترائِي بيد من تعصيه. هـ. وفي تمثيله بالحجر إشارة إلى قساوة قلبه ويبوسة طبعه، فلا يرجى منه خير قط. والعياذ بالله.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى ضد هؤلاء، وهم المخلصون، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تُبْطِلُوا صَدَقاتِكُمْ بِالْمَنِّ وَالْأَذى كَالَّذِي يُنْفِقُ مالَهُ رِئاءَ النَّاسِ وَلا يُؤْمِنُ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ فَمَثَلُهُ كَمَثَلِ صَفْوانٍ عَلَيْهِ تُرابٌ فَأَصابَهُ وابِلٌ فَتَرَكَهُ صَلْداً لا يَقْدِرُونَ عَلى شَيْءٍ مِمَّا كَسَبُوا وَاللَّهُ لا يَهْدِي الْقَوْمَ الْكافِرِينَ (264)","quran_en":"264. You who believe, do not nullify your charities through reminders and injury, like one who spends his wealth to be seen by people and does not believe in God and the Last Day. His likeness is a smooth rock with soil upon it: a downpour strikes it and leaves it bare. They gain nothing from what they have earned. God does not guide the unbelieving people.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The comparison and the bare stone","html":"<p>I say: the <i>kāf<\/i> in “like the one” is accusative as an expression qualifying an absolute accusative: a nullification like that of one who spends his wealth to be seen by people. Alternatively, it is circumstantial: resembling the one who spends for display. “To be seen” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">رئاء<\/bdi>) is an accusative of purpose. <i>Ṣafwān<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صفوان<\/bdi>) is a smooth stone; <i>ṣald<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صلد<\/bdi>), bare and uncovered by soil. The pronoun in “they gain nothing” is plural in accordance with the meaning of “the one,” which denotes the class.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the kāf in “like the one” is accusative as an expression qualifying an absolute accusative: a nullification like that of one who spends his wealth to be seen by people. Alternatively, it is circumstantial: resembling the one who spends for display. “To be seen” ( رئاء ) is an accusative of purpose. Ṣafwān ( صفوان ) is a smooth stone; ṣald ( صلد ), bare and uncovered by soil. The pronoun in “they gain nothing” is plural in accordance with the meaning of “the one,” which denotes the class."},{"type":"exposition","title":"A charitable appearance stripped away","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you who believe, do not nullify the reward of your charity by reminding its recipient of it or injuring him—for example, by mentioning it to other people. Your charity would then be void, like that of someone who spends his wealth to be seen by people and does not believe in God and the Last Day. His recompense on the Day of Resurrection becomes scattered dust.<\/p><p>His worldly benefit from charity, the concealment it affords him here and his exposure at the Resurrection resemble a smooth stone covered in soil. An observer supposes it to be good ground fit for cultivation. Then a heavy downpour strikes it and leaves it a dry, bare stone, free of soil. Likewise, those who make a display of their deeds benefit in this world through people’s praise and the concealment of their condition. When they arrive at the Resurrection, they find the deeds void and cannot benefit from anything they earned.<\/p><p>God does not guide the unbelieving people to their right course and the well-being of their religion. This indirectly warns that display, reminders and injury are unbelieving characteristics, which the believer must avoid. And success is through God.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you who believe, do not nullify the reward of your charity by reminding its recipient of it or injuring him—for example, by mentioning it to other people. Your charity would then be void, like that of someone who spends his wealth to be seen by people and does not believe in God and the Last Day. His recompense on the Day of Resurrection becomes scattered dust. His worldly benefit from charity, the concealment it affords him here and his exposure at the Resurrection resemble a smooth stone covered in soil. An observer supposes it to be good ground fit for cultivation. Then a heavy downpour strikes it and leaves it a dry, bare stone, free of soil. Likewise, those who make a display of their deeds benefit in this world through people’s praise and the concealment of their condition. When they arrive at the Resurrection, they find the deeds void and cannot benefit from anything they earned. God does not guide the unbelieving people to their right course and the well-being of their religion. This indirectly warns that display, reminders and injury are unbelieving characteristics, which the believer must avoid. And success is through God."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The inward conditions of pure action","html":"<p>The purification of deeds corresponds to the purification of hearts; the purification of hearts to watchfulness before the Knower of the unseen; watchfulness to gnosis; gnosis to witnessing; and witnessing is attained according to struggle. “Those who struggle for Us, We shall guide them to Our paths” [29:69].<\/p><p>The <i>Ḥikam<\/i> says: “Good deeds are the fruits of good states; good states come from realization in the stations of alighting (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقامات الإنزال<\/bdi>).” In sum, someone who has not realized the station of effacement cannot have works altogether free from an admixture of defect. One who has realized passing away ascribes to himself neither giving nor withholding, movement nor stillness. Nor does he see another as existent, so as to hope for benefit or good from him.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-243\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B217]<\/a><\/p><p>One of the allusive sayings declares: “You who act for show: the affairs of the person before whom you display yourself are in the hand of the One you disobey.” The comparison with a stone indicates hardness of heart and dryness of disposition. No good at all is to be expected from such a person. God is our refuge.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The purification of deeds corresponds to the purification of hearts; the purification of hearts to watchfulness before the Knower of the unseen; watchfulness to gnosis; gnosis to witnessing; and witnessing is attained according to struggle. “Those who struggle for Us, We shall guide them to Our paths” [29:69]. The Ḥikam says: “Good deeds are the fruits of good states; good states come from realization in the stations of alighting ( مقامات الإنزال ).” In sum, someone who has not realized the station of effacement cannot have works altogether free from an admixture of defect. One who has realized passing away ascribes to himself neither giving nor withholding, movement nor stillness. Nor does he see another as existent, so as to hope for benefit or good from him. [B217] One of the allusive sayings declares: “You who act for show: the affairs of the person before whom you display yourself are in the hand of the One you disobey.” The comparison with a stone indicates hardness of heart and dryness of disposition. No good at all is to be expected from such a person. God is our refuge."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then describes the opposite of these people: the sincere.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then describes the opposite of these people: the sincere."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2851,"arabic_characters":2199},{"id":"q2-265","q":"2:265","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:265 · The garden on rising ground","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 298–299","cairo":"I:298–299","source_page_first":298,"source_page_last":299,"ar":"وَمَثَلُ الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمُ ابْتِغاءَ مَرْضاتِ اللَّهِ وَتَثْبِيتاً مِنْ أَنْفُسِهِمْ كَمَثَلِ جَنَّةٍ بِرَبْوَةٍ أَصابَها وابِلٌ فَآتَتْ أُكُلَها ضِعْفَيْنِ فَإِنْ لَمْ يُصِبْها وابِلٌ فَطَلٌّ وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ (265)\nقلت: الربوة- مثلثة الراء-: المكان المرتفع، والوابل: المطر الغزير، والطل: المطر الخفيف، وفي ذلك يقول الراجز:\nوالطلُّ ما خفَّ من الأمْطَارِ ... والوابلُ الغزيرُ ذو أنْهِمَارِ\nو (ابتغاء مَرْضات الله) و (تثبيتاً) : حالان من الواو في: (ينفقون) ، أو مفعولان له. والتثبيت بمعنى التثبت، أي:\nالتحقق، كقوله تعالى: وَتَبَتَّلْ إِلَيْهِ تَبْتِيلًا أي: تبتلاً.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: مَثَلُ الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمُ في سبيل الله ابْتِغاءَ مَرْضاتِ اللَّهِ وتحققاً مِنْ أَنْفُسِهِمْ بثواب الله، أو تحقيقاً من أنفسهم بالوصول إلى رضوان الله إن بذلوا أموالهم في طلب رضى الله، مَثَلُ نفقتهم في النمو والارتفاع كَمَثَلِ جَنَّةٍ أي: بستان بِرَبْوَةٍ بمكان مرتفع، فإن شجره يكون أحسن منظراً وأزكى ثمراً، أَصابَها وابِلٌ أي: مطر غزير فَآتَتْ أُكُلَها أي: ثمارها ضِعْفَيْنِ أي: مِثْلَيْ ما كانت تثمر في عادتها، أي: حملت في سَنَةٍ ما يحمل غيرها في سنتين، بسبب هذا المطر الذي نزل بها، فَإِنْ لَمْ يُصِبْها وابِلٌ فَطَلٌّ أي: فيصيبها طل، أي: مطر قليل يكفيها لطيب تربتها وارتفاع مكانها، فأقلُّ شيء يكفيها.\nوالمراد: أن نفقات هؤلاء، لإخلاصهم وكمال يقينهم، كثيرة زاكية عند الله، وإن كانت قليلة في الحس فهي كثيرة في المعنى. وفي الحديث: «مَنْ تصدَّقَ ولو بلُقْمة وقعتْ في كَفٍّ الرحمن فيُرَبِّيها كما يُربى أحدُكُم فلُوَّهُ أو فَصِيله(1) ، حتى تكونَ مثل الجبل» . وفي قوله: وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ: تحذير من الرياء، وترغيب فى الإخلاص. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: تنمية الأعمال على قدر تصفية الأحوال، وتصفية الأحوال على قدر التحقق بمقامات الإنزال، أي:\nعلى قدر التحقق بالإنزال في مقامات اليقين، فكل من تحقق بالنزول في مقامات اليقين، ورسخت قدمه فيها، كانت أعماله كلها عظيمة، مضاعفة أضعافاً كثيرة، فتسبيحة واحدة من العارف، أو تهليلة واحدة، تعدل الوجود بأسره، ولا يزنها ميزان، وكذلك سائر أعمال العارف: كلها عظيمة مضاعفة لأنها بالله ومن الله وإلى الله، وما كان بالله ومن الله لا يطرقه نقص ولا يشوبه خلل، ولأجل هذا صارت أوقاتهم كلها ليلة القدر، وأماكنهم كلها عرفات، وأنفاسهم كلها زكيات، وصحبتهم كلها نفحات، ومخالطتهم كلها بركات. نفعنا الله بذكرهم وخرطنا في سلكهم. آمين.\nثم حذَّر الحق تعالى من طوارق الخلل بعد تمام العمل، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمَثَلُ الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمُ ابْتِغاءَ مَرْضاتِ اللَّهِ وَتَثْبِيتاً مِنْ أَنْفُسِهِمْ كَمَثَلِ جَنَّةٍ بِرَبْوَةٍ أَصابَها وابِلٌ فَآتَتْ أُكُلَها ضِعْفَيْنِ فَإِنْ لَمْ يُصِبْها وابِلٌ فَطَلٌّ وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ (265)","quran_en":"265. The likeness of those who spend their wealth seeking God’s good pleasure and from firm conviction within themselves is a garden on rising ground. A downpour strikes it and it yields its produce twice over. If no downpour strikes it, a light rain suffices. God sees what you do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The elevated garden and the two rains","html":"<p>I say: <i>rabwa<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ربوة<\/bdi>), whose <i>rāʾ<\/i> may take any of the three short vowels, is elevated ground. <i>Wābil<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وابل<\/bdi>) is abundant rain; <i>ṭall<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">طل<\/bdi>), light rain. The poet in <i>rajaz<\/i> metre says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Ṭall is the light kind of rain;<br>wābil is abundant, pouring rain.<\/blockquote><p>“Seeking God’s good pleasure” and “from firm conviction” are either circumstantial expressions referring to the subject of “spend,” or accusatives of purpose. <i>Tathbīt<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تثبيت<\/bdi>) here means <i>tathabbut<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تثبت<\/bdi>), being firmly convinced or assured, as “devote yourself to Him with complete devotion” [73:8] uses <i>tabtīl<\/i> where <i>tabattul<\/i> is meant.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: rabwa ( ربوة ), whose rāʾ may take any of the three short vowels, is elevated ground. Wābil ( وابل ) is abundant rain; ṭall ( طل ), light rain. The poet in rajaz metre says: Ṭall is the light kind of rain; wābil is abundant, pouring rain. “Seeking God’s good pleasure” and “from firm conviction” are either circumstantial expressions referring to the subject of “spend,” or accusatives of purpose. Tathbīt ( تثبيت ) here means tathabbut ( تثبت ), being firmly convinced or assured, as “devote yourself to Him with complete devotion” [73:8] uses tabtīl where tabattul is meant."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The growth of a gift given with certainty","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the likeness of those who spend their wealth in God’s path seeking His good pleasure, inwardly certain of His reward—or assured that giving their wealth in pursuit of His approval leads to His good pleasure—is, in growth and increase, a garden upon rising ground. Its trees are more beautiful to behold and its fruit more abundant.<\/p><p>A downpour strikes it, and it yields twice its ordinary produce: in one year it bears what another garden bears in two, because of the rain. If no downpour strikes it, light rain suffices because of its good soil and elevated position. The least thing is enough for it.<\/p><p>The meaning is that, because of their sincerity and perfect certainty, these people’s gifts are abundant and fruitful before God. Though small in sensory measure, they are great in inward meaning. The hadith says: “Whoever gives in charity, even a morsel, it falls into the palm of the All-Merciful, who nurtures it as one of you nurtures his foal or young camel, until it becomes like a mountain.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-244\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B218]<\/a> “God sees what you do” warns against display and encourages sincerity. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the likeness of those who spend their wealth in God’s path seeking His good pleasure, inwardly certain of His reward—or assured that giving their wealth in pursuit of His approval leads to His good pleasure—is, in growth and increase, a garden upon rising ground. Its trees are more beautiful to behold and its fruit more abundant. A downpour strikes it, and it yields twice its ordinary produce: in one year it bears what another garden bears in two, because of the rain. If no downpour strikes it, light rain suffices because of its good soil and elevated position. The least thing is enough for it. The meaning is that, because of their sincerity and perfect certainty, these people’s gifts are abundant and fruitful before God. Though small in sensory measure, they are great in inward meaning. The hadith says: “Whoever gives in charity, even a morsel, it falls into the palm of the All-Merciful, who nurtures it as one of you nurtures his foal or young camel, until it becomes like a mountain.” [B218] “God sees what you do” warns against display and encourages sincerity. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The measure of action is the station from which it comes","html":"<p>The growth of deeds corresponds to the purification of states, and the purification of states to realization in the stations of alighting: that is, realizing one’s alighting in the stations of certainty. Whoever is established in these stations, with firm footing in them, has all his deeds made great and multiplied many times.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-245\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B217]<\/a><\/p><p>A single glorification or declaration of unity from a gnostic equals the whole of existence and cannot be weighed by a balance. So too all his other works are great and multiplied, for they are through God, from God and towards God. What is through God and from God is not visited by deficiency or mixed with defect.<\/p><p>For this reason all their times become the Night of Power; all their places, ʿArafāt; all their breaths, pure; all their companionship, breaths of divine grace; all association with them, blessings. May God benefit us through remembering them and thread us into their company. Amen.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The growth of deeds corresponds to the purification of states, and the purification of states to realization in the stations of alighting: that is, realizing one’s alighting in the stations of certainty. Whoever is established in these stations, with firm footing in them, has all his deeds made great and multiplied many times. [B217] A single glorification or declaration of unity from a gnostic equals the whole of existence and cannot be weighed by a balance. So too all his other works are great and multiplied, for they are through God, from God and towards God. What is through God and from God is not visited by deficiency or mixed with defect. For this reason all their times become the Night of Power; all their places, ʿArafāt; all their breaths, pure; all their companionship, breaths of divine grace; all association with them, blessings. May God benefit us through remembering them and thread us into their company. Amen."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then warns against the intrusion of defects after a work has been completed.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then warns against the intrusion of defects after a work has been completed."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C299.1","page":299,"ar":"الفلو: هو المهر الصغير، والفصيل: ولد الناقة بعد أن يفصل عن أمه.","en":"A falūw is a young foal; a faṣīl is a she-camel’s young after it has been separated from its mother.","anchor":"فلُوَّهُ أو فَصِيله"}],"english_characters":2752,"arabic_characters":2254},{"id":"q2-266","q":"2:266","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:266 · The garden destroyed when it is needed most","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 299–300","cairo":"I:299–300","source_page_first":299,"source_page_last":300,"ar":"أَيَوَدُّ أَحَدُكُمْ أَنْ تَكُونَ لَهُ جَنَّةٌ مِنْ نَخِيلٍ وَأَعْنابٍ تَجْرِي مِنْ تَحْتِهَا الْأَنْهارُ لَهُ فِيها مِنْ كُلِّ الثَّمَراتِ وَأَصابَهُ الْكِبَرُ وَلَهُ ذُرِّيَّةٌ ضُعَفاءُ فَأَصابَها إِعْصارٌ فِيهِ نارٌ فَاحْتَرَقَتْ كَذلِكَ يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ لَكُمُ الْآياتِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَتَفَكَّرُونَ (266)\nقلت: الإعصار: عمود من ريح فيه عجاجة، يدور ويرتفع.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: أيتمنى أحدكم أَنْ تَكُونَ لَهُ جَنَّةٌ أي: بستان مِنْ نَخِيلٍ وَأَعْنابٍ، هما الغالبان فيه لكثرة منافعهما، تَجْرِي مِنْ تحت تلك الأشجار الْأَنْهارُ إذ من كمال البستان أن يشتمل على الماء البارد والظل الممدود، ولَهُ فِيها أي: في تلك الجنة مِنْ كُلِّ الثَّمَراتِ زائدة على النخيل والأعناب، ثم أَصابَهُ الْكِبَرُ فضعف عن القيام بتلك الجنة، وَلَهُ ذُرِّيَّةٌ ضُعَفاءُ لا يستطيعون القيام بأنفسهم لصغرهم، فأصاب تلك الجنة إِعْصارٌ أي: ريح شديد فِيهِ نارٌ فَاحْتَرَقَتْ تلك الجنة، فلا تسأل عن حسرة صاحب هذا البستان، لخوفه من ضياع نفسه وعياله. وهذا مثال لمن يُكثر من أعمال البر، كالصلاة والصيام والصدقة والحج والجهاد وغير ذلك، ثم يُعجب به، ويفتخر وَيَمُنَّ بصدقته أو يؤذي، فتحبط تلك الأعمال وتذهب، فيتحسر عليها يوم القيامة، وهو أحوج ما يكون إليها. أو يعمل بالطاعة في أيام عمره، فإذا قرب الموت عمل بالمعاصي حتى ختم له بها فحبطت تلك الأعمال، والعياذ بالله كَذلِكَ يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ لَكُمُ الْآياتِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَتَفَكَّرُونَ فيها فتعتبرون، وتخلصون في أعمالكم، وتخافون من سوء عاقبتكم. أعاذنا الله من ذلك.\nالإشارة: في الآية تخويف للمريد أن يرجع إلى عوائده، ويلتفت إلى عوالم حسه، فيشتغل بالدنيا بعد أن استشرف على جنة المعارف، تجري على قلبه أنهار العلوم، فينقُضُ العهد مع شيخه، أو يسيىء الأدب معه، ولم يتب حتى تيبس أشجار معارفه، وتلعب به ريح الهوى، فيحترق قلبه بنار الشهوات.\nقال البيضاوي: وأشبههم به من جال سره في عالم الملكوت، وترقى بفكره إلى جناب الجبروت، ثم نكص على عقبيه إلى عالم الزور، والتفت إلى ما سوى الحق وجعل سعيه هباء منثوراً. هـ.\nثم رغّب الحق تعالى فى الصدقة من الكسب الطيب، فرضا ونفلا، فقال:","quran_ar":"أَيَوَدُّ أَحَدُكُمْ أَنْ تَكُونَ لَهُ جَنَّةٌ مِنْ نَخِيلٍ وَأَعْنابٍ تَجْرِي مِنْ تَحْتِهَا الْأَنْهارُ لَهُ فِيها مِنْ كُلِّ الثَّمَراتِ وَأَصابَهُ الْكِبَرُ وَلَهُ ذُرِّيَّةٌ ضُعَفاءُ فَأَصابَها إِعْصارٌ فِيهِ نارٌ فَاحْتَرَقَتْ كَذلِكَ يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ لَكُمُ الْآياتِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَتَفَكَّرُونَ (266)","quran_en":"266. Would any of you wish to have a garden of palms and vines, with rivers flowing beneath it and every kind of fruit within it, while old age has overtaken him and his offspring are weak—then a whirlwind containing fire strikes it and it burns? Thus God makes the signs clear to you, so that you may reflect.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The whirlwind","html":"<p>I say: an <i>iʿṣār<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إعصار<\/bdi>) is a column of wind containing dust, revolving and rising.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: an iʿṣār ( إعصار ) is a column of wind containing dust, revolving and rising."},{"type":"exposition","title":"A lifetime’s works lost at its end","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: would any of you wish to have a garden of palms and vines—its predominant trees because of their many benefits—with rivers flowing beneath them? A perfect garden includes cool water and extended shade. Within it he has every kind of fruit besides palms and vines. Then old age overtakes him, weakening his ability to tend it, and he has young, weak children who cannot look after themselves. A strong whirlwind containing fire strikes the garden and burns it.<\/p><p>Do not ask how great the owner’s grief would be, fearing ruin for himself and his dependants. This illustrates someone who performs abundant good works—prayer, fasting, charity, pilgrimage, fighting in God’s cause and others—then admires and boasts of them, or reminds recipients of his charity and injures them. The works become void and disappear, and he grieves over them at the Resurrection when he most needs them. Or he acts obediently throughout his life, then near death acts disobediently and ends in that state, so that the works are lost. God is our refuge.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-246\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B219]<\/a><\/p><p>Thus God makes the signs clear so that you may reflect, take a lesson, act sincerely and fear an evil end. May God preserve us from that.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: would any of you wish to have a garden of palms and vines—its predominant trees because of their many benefits—with rivers flowing beneath them? A perfect garden includes cool water and extended shade. Within it he has every kind of fruit besides palms and vines. Then old age overtakes him, weakening his ability to tend it, and he has young, weak children who cannot look after themselves. A strong whirlwind containing fire strikes the garden and burns it. Do not ask how great the owner’s grief would be, fearing ruin for himself and his dependants. This illustrates someone who performs abundant good works—prayer, fasting, charity, pilgrimage, fighting in God’s cause and others—then admires and boasts of them, or reminds recipients of his charity and injures them. The works become void and disappear, and he grieves over them at the Resurrection when he most needs them. Or he acts obediently throughout his life, then near death acts disobediently and ends in that state, so that the works are lost. God is our refuge. [B219] Thus God makes the signs clear so that you may reflect, take a lesson, act sincerely and fear an evil end. May God preserve us from that."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Returning to habit after approaching the garden of gnosis","html":"<p>The verse warns the disciple against returning to his habitual ways and looking back towards the worlds of sensation, becoming occupied with the world after looking out upon the garden of gnoses, with rivers of knowledge flowing through his heart. He breaks the covenant with his shaykh or behaves improperly towards him, and does not repent until the trees of his gnosis wither. The wind of desire plays with him, and his heart burns in the fire of appetites.<\/p><p>Al-Bayḍāwī said: the person who most resembles this is one whose secret roamed the unseen kingdom and whose thought ascended to the precinct of divine omnipotence, then turned back towards the world of falsehood, looked to other than the Real and made his striving scattered dust.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The verse warns the disciple against returning to his habitual ways and looking back towards the worlds of sensation, becoming occupied with the world after looking out upon the garden of gnoses, with rivers of knowledge flowing through his heart. He breaks the covenant with his shaykh or behaves improperly towards him, and does not repent until the trees of his gnosis wither. The wind of desire plays with him, and his heart burns in the fire of appetites. Al-Bayḍāwī said: the person who most resembles this is one whose secret roamed the unseen kingdom and whose thought ascended to the precinct of divine omnipotence, then turned back towards the world of falsehood, looked to other than the Real and made his striving scattered dust."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then encourages giving charity from good earnings, whether obligatory or voluntary.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then encourages giving charity from good earnings, whether obligatory or voluntary."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2131,"arabic_characters":1867},{"id":"q2-267","q":"2:267","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:267 · Give what is good, and seek knowledge through a sound spiritual state","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 300–302","cairo":"I:300–302","source_page_first":300,"source_page_last":302,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا أَنْفِقُوا مِنْ طَيِّباتِ ما كَسَبْتُمْ وَمِمَّا أَخْرَجْنا لَكُمْ مِنَ الْأَرْضِ وَلا تَيَمَّمُوا الْخَبِيثَ مِنْهُ تُنْفِقُونَ وَلَسْتُمْ بِآخِذِيهِ إِلاَّ أَنْ تُغْمِضُوا فِيهِ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ غَنِيٌّ حَمِيدٌ (267)\nقلت: (تيمموا) : أصله: تتيمموا، أي تقصدوا، وجملة (تنفقون) : حال مقدرة- من فاعل (تيمموا) ، و (منه) :\nيصح أن يتعلق ب- (تنفقون) أو ب- (الخبيث) ، أي: ولا تقصدوا الخبيث حال كونكم تنفقونه، أو لا تقصدوا الخبيث تنفقون منه، و (لستم بآخذيه) : حال أيضًا من فاعل (تنفقون) .\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا أَنْفِقُوا مِنْ طَيِّباتِ ما كَسَبْتُمْ من الأموال في التجارة وغيرها، وفي الحديث عنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال: «يا معشرَ التُجار، أنتم فُجار إلا من اتقى وبَرَّ وصَدَق وَقَالَ بالمال(1) هكذا وهكذا» .\nوقوله مِنْ طَيِّباتِ ما كَسَبْتُمْ أي: من حلاله، أو من خياره، أما في الزكاة فعلى الوجوب، إذ لا يصح دفع الرديء فيها، وأما فى التطوع فعلى سبيل الكمال، وأنفقوا أيضاً من طيبات ما أَخْرَجْنا لَكُمْ مِنَ الْأَرْضِ من أنواع الحبوب والثمار والفواكه، وفي الحديث عنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «مَا مِنْ مُسْلِم يَغْرِسَ غَرْساً، أَوْ يَزرَعُ زَرْعاً، فَيَأكُلُ مِنهُ إنْسَانٌ ولا دَابَّةٌ ولاَ طَائِرٌ، إلاَّ كَانَت لَهُ صدقةً إلى يَوَمِ الِقِيَامِةِ» . ولا تقصدوا الْخَبِيثَ أي: الرديء من أموالكم، فتنفقون منه وأنتم لَسْتُمْ بِآخِذِيهِ في ديونكم إِلَّا أَنْ تُغْمِضُوا بصركم فيه، وتقبضونه حياء أو كرهاً أو مسامحة.\nنزلت في قوم كانوا يتصدقون بخبيث التمر وشراره، فنُهوا عنه، وأدبهم بقوله: وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ غَنِيٌّ عن إنفاقكم، وإنما أمركم به منفعة لكم، حَمِيدٌ بقبوله وإثابته، فهو فعيل بمعنى فاعل، مبالغة، أي: يحمد فعلكم ويشكره لكم، إن أحسنتم فيه، وفي الحديث عن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال: «إِنَّ اللهَ قَسَم بينَكم أخلاقكم كما قسم أرزاقكم، وإن الله طيِّبٌ لا يقبلُ إلا طيباً، لا يكسبُ عبدٌ مالاً من حرام فيتصدقُ منه فيُقْبل منه، ولا ينفقُ منه فيبَارك له فيه، ولا يتركهُ خلفَ ظهره إلا كان زاده إلى النار، وإن الله لا يمحو السيّء بالسيىء ولكن يمحو السيء بالحسن، وإن الخبيث لا يمحوه الخبيث» .\nالإشارة: يا أيها الذين آمنوا إيمان الخصوص، أنفقوا العلوم اللدنية والأسرار الربانية، من طيبات ما كسبتم من تصفية أسراركم وتزكية أرواحكم، وأنفقوا أيضاً علوم الشريعة وأنوار الطريقة، مما أخرجنا لكم من أرض نفوسكم التي تزكت بالأعمال الصافية والأحوال المُرْضِية.\nولا تيمموا العمل الخبيث أو الحال الخبيث، تريدون أن تنفقوا منه شيئاً من تلك العلوم، فإن ذلك لا يزيد النفس إلا جهلاً وبعداً، فكما أن الحبة لا تنبت إلا في الأرض الطيبة، كذلك النفس لا تُدفن إلا في الحالة المرضية، فلا تؤخذ العلوم اللدنية من النفس حتى تدفن في أرض الخُمول، وأرض الخمول هي الأحوال المرضية، الموافقة للقواعد الشرعية، وإليه الإشارة بقوله: وَلَسْتُمْ بِآخِذِيهِ أي: لستم بآخذي العلم اللدني من الحال الخبيث، إلا أن تغيبوا فيه عن حِسَّكم، ومن غلبه الحال لم يبق عليه مقال. وعليها تتخرج قصة لص الحَمَّام(2) ، فلا يقتدى به لغلبة الحال عليه، واعلموا أن الله غني حميد، لا يتقرب إليه إلا بما هو حميد. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم حذّر من الشّحّ، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا أَنْفِقُوا مِنْ طَيِّباتِ ما كَسَبْتُمْ وَمِمَّا أَخْرَجْنا لَكُمْ مِنَ الْأَرْضِ وَلا تَيَمَّمُوا الْخَبِيثَ مِنْهُ تُنْفِقُونَ وَلَسْتُمْ بِآخِذِيهِ إِلاَّ أَنْ تُغْمِضُوا فِيهِ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ غَنِيٌّ حَمِيدٌ (267)","quran_en":"267. You who believe, spend from the good things you have earned and from what We have brought forth for you from the earth. Do not deliberately select the bad to spend, when you would not accept it yourselves unless you overlooked its defects. Know that God is Independent, Praiseworthy.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Intention and the circumstantial clauses","html":"<p>I say: <i>tayammamū<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تيمموا<\/bdi>) originally has two initial <i>tāʾs<\/i>: <i>tatayammamū<\/i>, “intend” or “deliberately seek.” “You spend” is an anticipated circumstance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال مقدرة<\/bdi>) referring to its subject. “From it” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">منه<\/bdi>) may attach to “spend” or “the bad”: do not select the bad, intending to spend it; or do not select bad property from which to spend. “You would not accept it” is likewise a circumstance referring to the subject of “spend.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: tayammamū ( تيمموا ) originally has two initial tāʾs : tatayammamū , “intend” or “deliberately seek.” “You spend” is an anticipated circumstance ( حال مقدرة ) referring to its subject. “From it” ( منه ) may attach to “spend” or “the bad”: do not select the bad, intending to spend it; or do not select bad property from which to spend. “You would not accept it” is likewise a circumstance referring to the subject of “spend.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Lawful earnings and acceptable quality","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you who believe, spend from the good property you earn through trade and other means. The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Company of traders, you are sinful except those who are mindful of God, act uprightly, speak truthfully and distribute wealth this way and that.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-247\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B220]<\/a><\/p><p>“From the good things you have earned” means their lawful portion, or their good quality. In obligatory alms this is binding, since giving inferior goods is not valid; in voluntary charity it is a matter of perfection. Spend also from the good things We have brought forth from the earth: the different grains, crops and fruits. The Prophet said: “No Muslim plants a tree or sows a crop from which a human, animal or bird eats without it being a charity for him until the Day of Resurrection.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-248\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B221]<\/a><\/p><p>Do not deliberately select the bad—the inferior part of your property—to spend, when you would not accept it in payment of debts unless you closed your eyes to it and took it through embarrassment, reluctance or indulgence. The verse was revealed concerning people who gave bad and inferior dates in charity; they were forbidden to do so.<\/p><p>He disciplines them with: “Know that God is Independent.” He needs none of your expenditure; He commands it only for your benefit. He praises you by accepting and rewarding it. Here <i>ḥamīd<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حميد<\/bdi>), on the pattern <i>faʿīl<\/i>, has the intensive sense of an active participle: He commends and thanks you for your deed if you perform it well.<\/p><p>The Messenger of God is quoted as saying: “God has apportioned your characters as He apportioned your provisions. God is good and accepts only what is good. A servant does not earn unlawful wealth and give charity from it with acceptance, nor spend from it with blessing. If he leaves it behind him, it becomes his provision for the Fire. God does not erase evil through evil, but erases evil through good; the impure does not erase the impure.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-249\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B222]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you who believe, spend from the good property you earn through trade and other means. The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Company of traders, you are sinful except those who are mindful of God, act uprightly, speak truthfully and distribute wealth this way and that.” [B220] “From the good things you have earned” means their lawful portion, or their good quality. In obligatory alms this is binding, since giving inferior goods is not valid; in voluntary charity it is a matter of perfection. Spend also from the good things We have brought forth from the earth: the different grains, crops and fruits. The Prophet said: “No Muslim plants a tree or sows a crop from which a human, animal or bird eats without it being a charity for him until the Day of Resurrection.” [B221] Do not deliberately select the bad—the inferior part of your property—to spend, when you would not accept it in payment of debts unless you closed your eyes to it and took it through embarrassment, reluctance or indulgence. The verse was revealed concerning people who gave bad and inferior dates in charity; they were forbidden to do so. He disciplines them with: “Know that God is Independent.” He needs none of your expenditure; He commands it only for your benefit. He praises you by accepting and rewarding it. Here ḥamīd ( حميد ), on the pattern faʿīl , has the intensive sense of an active participle: He commends and thanks you for your deed if you perform it well. The Messenger of God is quoted as saying: “God has apportioned your characters as He apportioned your provisions. God is good and accepts only what is good. A servant does not earn unlawful wealth and give charity from it with acceptance, nor spend from it with blessing. If he leaves it behind him, it becomes his provision for the Fire. God does not erase evil through evil, but erases evil through good; the impure does not erase the impure.” [B222]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"A sound state is the soil of sound knowledge","html":"<p>You who believe with the faith of the elect: spend the divinely bestowed sciences and lordly mysteries from the good fruits of purifying your secrets and refining your spirits. Spend also the sciences of Sacred Law and the lights of the Path, from what We have brought forth from the earth of your selves, purified by pure deeds and pleasing states.<\/p><p>Do not seek an impure deed or impure state, intending to impart any of these sciences from it. That only increases the self’s ignorance and distance. As a seed grows only in good earth, so the self is to be buried only in an approved state. Divinely bestowed knowledge is not drawn from the self until it has been buried in the earth of obscurity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أرض الخمول<\/bdi>). The earth of obscurity is the approved states that accord with the principles of the Sacred Law.<\/p><p>This is indicated by “you would not accept it”: you do not receive divinely bestowed knowledge from an impure state, unless you have become absent within it from your sensory awareness. One overcome by a state is beyond reproach for that state. On this basis the story of the thief at the bathhouse is explained: he is not to be imitated, because a state had overcome him.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-250\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B223]<\/a> Know that God is Independent, Praiseworthy; one draws near to Him only through what is praiseworthy. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"You who believe with the faith of the elect: spend the divinely bestowed sciences and lordly mysteries from the good fruits of purifying your secrets and refining your spirits. Spend also the sciences of Sacred Law and the lights of the Path, from what We have brought forth from the earth of your selves, purified by pure deeds and pleasing states. Do not seek an impure deed or impure state, intending to impart any of these sciences from it. That only increases the self’s ignorance and distance. As a seed grows only in good earth, so the self is to be buried only in an approved state. Divinely bestowed knowledge is not drawn from the self until it has been buried in the earth of obscurity ( أرض الخمول ). The earth of obscurity is the approved states that accord with the principles of the Sacred Law. This is indicated by “you would not accept it”: you do not receive divinely bestowed knowledge from an impure state, unless you have become absent within it from your sensory awareness. One overcome by a state is beyond reproach for that state. On this basis the story of the thief at the bathhouse is explained: he is not to be imitated, because a state had overcome him. [B223] Know that God is Independent, Praiseworthy; one draws near to Him only through what is praiseworthy. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then warns against avarice.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then warns against avarice."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C301.1","page":301,"ar":"أي: صرف المال في وجوه الخير، قال ابن الأثير: العرب تجعل القول عبارة عن جميع الأفعال، وتطلقه على غير الكلام واللسان. فتقول: قال بيده، أي: أخذه. وقال برجله، أي: مشى. وكل ذلك على المجاز.","en":"That is, he spent wealth on good causes. Ibn al-Athīr said: the Arabs use “saying” to express all kinds of actions, applying it to things other than speech and the tongue. Thus they say “he said with his hand,” meaning he took it, and “he said with his foot,” meaning he walked. All of this is figurative.","anchor":"وَقَالَ بالمال"},{"id":"C301.2","page":301,"ar":"وهو رجل عرف بالزهد وأقبل الناس عليه، فدخل حماما ولبس ثياب غيره، وخرج، فوقف في الطريق حتى عرفه الناس، فأخذوه وضربوه، واستردوا الثياب وهجروه. قلت: ما فعل هذا الرجل مبالغة وشطط لا يقره الشرع. وكما قال المصنف: لا يقتدى به لغلبة الحال عليه. والقصة ذكرها الغزالي في الإحياء ٣/٣٠٥، وابن عباد في شرح الحكم ١/٨٠.","en":"He was a man known for asceticism to whom people flocked. He entered a bathhouse, put on someone else’s clothes, went out and stood in the road until people recognised him. They seized and beat him, recovered the clothes and shunned him. I say: what this man did was an excess and a departure from moderation that the Sacred Law does not approve. As the author says, he is not to be followed, because his spiritual state had overwhelmed him. Al-Ghazālī recounts the story in the Iḥyāʾ, 3:305, and Ibn ʿAbbād in his commentary on the Ḥikam, 1:80.","anchor":"قصة لص الحَمَّام"}],"english_characters":3723,"arabic_characters":2860},{"id":"q2-268","q":"2:268","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:268 · The threat of poverty and the promise of grace","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 302–303","cairo":"I:302–303","source_page_first":302,"source_page_last":303,"ar":"الشَّيْطانُ يَعِدُكُمُ الْفَقْرَ وَيَأْمُرُكُمْ بِالْفَحْشاءِ وَاللَّهُ يَعِدُكُمْ مَغْفِرَةً مِنْهُ وَفَضْلاً وَاللَّهُ واسِعٌ عَلِيمٌ (268)\nقلت: يقال: وعدته خيراً ووعدته شرّاً، هذا إن ذكر الخير أو الشر، وأما إذا لم يذكر فيقال في الخير: وعدته، وفى الشر: أوعدته، قال الشاعر:\nوإنّيَ وإنْ أوْعَدْتُهُ أوْ وَعَدْتُهُ ... لمخلِفُ إيعادي ومنْجِزُ مَوْعدي(1)\nو (الفحشاء) هنا: البخل والشح.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: الشَّيْطانُ يَعِدُكُمُ أي: يخوفكم الْفَقْرَ بسبب الإنفاق، ويقول في وسوسته: إن أعطيت مالك بقيت فقيراً تتكفف الناس، وَيَأْمُرُكُمْ بِالْفَحْشاءِ أي: ويأمركم بالبخل والشح، والعرب تسمي البخيل فاحشاً، وفي الحديث: «البخيلُ بعيدٌ من اللهِ، بعيدُ من الناسِ، بَعِيدٌ مِنَ الجَنَّةِ قَرِيبٌ من النَّارِ. والسخي قَرِيبٌ مِنَ اللهِ. قَرِيبٌ من النَّاس، قريبٌ من الجَنَّةِ، بَعِيدٌ من النارِ. ولجَاهلٌ سَخِيٌ أَحَبُ إلى الله من عابدٍ بخيل» .\nوفي حديث آخر: «إنَّ الله يأخذُ بيد السخيِّ كلما عثر» . وَاللَّهُ يَعِدُكُمْ في الإنفاق مَغْفِرَةً مِنْهُ لذنوبكم، وستراً لعيوبكم، وَفَضْلًا أي: خَلَفاً أفضل مما أنفقتم في الدنيا والآخرة، وَما أَنْفَقْتُمْ مِنْ شَيْءٍ فَهُوَ يُخْلِفُهُ، وَاللَّهُ واسِعٌ الفضل والعطاء، عَلِيمٌ بما أنفقتم، ولماذا أنفقتم، وفيما أخلصتم، لا يخفى عليه شيء من أموركم.\nالإشارة: إذا توجه المريد إلى الله تعالى، وأراد سلوك طريق التجريد والزهد والانقطاع إلى الله تعالى، تعرض له الشيطان، اختباراً منه تعالى وابتلاء، إذ الحضرة محروسة بالقواطع ليظهر الصادق في الطلب من الكاذب، فيخوفه من الفقر، ويأمره بالوقوف مع الأسباب والعوائد، وهي أفحش المعاصي عند الخواص، إذ الهمة العالية تأنف عن الاشتغال بغير الحضرة الإلهية. والله يعدكم- أيها المتوجهون إليه- مغفرة لذنوبكم، وستراً لعيوبكم، فيغطي وصفكم بوصفه، ونعتكم بنعته، فيُوصلكم بما منه إليكم من الفضل والجود، لا بما منكم إليه من المجاهدة والمكابدة، وَلَوْلا فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَتُهُ مَا زَكى مِنْكُمْ مِنْ أَحَدٍ أَبَداً، (والله واسع) الجود والإحسان، (عليم) بمن يستحق الفضل والامتنان.\nومن نتائج الزهد والانقطاع: ورود الحكمة على لسان العبد وقلبه، كما أشار إلى ذلك بقوله:","quran_ar":"الشَّيْطانُ يَعِدُكُمُ الْفَقْرَ وَيَأْمُرُكُمْ بِالْفَحْشاءِ وَاللَّهُ يَعِدُكُمْ مَغْفِرَةً مِنْهُ وَفَضْلاً وَاللَّهُ واسِعٌ عَلِيمٌ (268)","quran_en":"268. Satan threatens you with poverty and commands you to indecency. God promises you forgiveness from Him and bounty. God is All-Encompassing, Knowing.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Promise, threat and indecency","html":"<p>I say: one says <i>waʿadtuhu khayran<\/i>, “I promised him good,” and <i>waʿadtuhu sharran<\/i>, “I threatened him with evil,” when the good or evil is specified. Without specifying it, <i>waʿadtuhu<\/i> denotes promising good, while <i>awʿadtuhu<\/i> denotes threatening evil. The poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Whether I threaten him or promise him,<br>I fail to carry out my threat and fulfil my promise.<\/blockquote><p>“Indecency” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفحشاء<\/bdi>) here means miserliness and avarice.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: one says waʿadtuhu khayran , “I promised him good,” and waʿadtuhu sharran , “I threatened him with evil,” when the good or evil is specified. Without specifying it, waʿadtuhu denotes promising good, while awʿadtuhu denotes threatening evil. The poet says: Whether I threaten him or promise him, I fail to carry out my threat and fulfil my promise. “Indecency” ( الفحشاء ) here means miserliness and avarice."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The whispering that restrains generosity","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Satan threatens you with poverty because of expenditure. In his whispering he says: “If you give away your property, you will become poor, holding out your hand to people.” He commands you to miserliness and avarice; the Arabs call a miser <i>fāḥish<\/i>. A report says: “The miser is far from God, far from people, far from the Garden and near the Fire. The generous person is near God, near people, near the Garden and far from the Fire. An ignorant but generous person is dearer to God than a miserly worshipper.” Another says: “God takes the generous person by the hand whenever he stumbles.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-251\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B224]<\/a><\/p><p>God promises you, through expenditure, forgiveness for your sins, concealment of your faults and bounty: a replacement better than what you spent, in this world and the Hereafter. “Whatever you spend, He replaces it” [34:39]. God is expansive in bounty and giving. He knows what you spend, why you spend it and where your sincerity lies. Nothing in your affairs is hidden from Him.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Satan threatens you with poverty because of expenditure. In his whispering he says: “If you give away your property, you will become poor, holding out your hand to people.” He commands you to miserliness and avarice; the Arabs call a miser fāḥish . A report says: “The miser is far from God, far from people, far from the Garden and near the Fire. The generous person is near God, near people, near the Garden and far from the Fire. An ignorant but generous person is dearer to God than a miserly worshipper.” Another says: “God takes the generous person by the hand whenever he stumbles.” [B224] God promises you, through expenditure, forgiveness for your sins, concealment of your faults and bounty: a replacement better than what you spent, in this world and the Hereafter. “Whatever you spend, He replaces it” [34:39]. God is expansive in bounty and giving. He knows what you spend, why you spend it and where your sincerity lies. Nothing in your affairs is hidden from Him."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The test on the path of detachment","html":"<p>When a disciple turns towards God Most High and wishes to travel the path of detachment, renunciation and complete devotion to Him, Satan confronts him as a test and trial from God. The Presence is guarded by obstacles, so that the truthful seeker may be distinguished from the false.<\/p><p>He frightens him with poverty and commands him to remain with causes and customary patterns—the most indecent disobedience in the eyes of the elect, for lofty aspiration disdains occupation with anything other than the divine Presence. God promises you who turn towards Him forgiveness of sins and concealment of faults. He covers your quality with His quality and your attribute with His attribute, bringing you to Himself through the bounty and generosity that come from Him to you, not through the struggle and endurance that come from you to Him. “Were it not for God’s bounty and mercy upon you, not one of you would ever become pure” [24:21]. God is expansive in generosity and goodness, knowing who is fit for bounty and grace.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"When a disciple turns towards God Most High and wishes to travel the path of detachment, renunciation and complete devotion to Him, Satan confronts him as a test and trial from God. The Presence is guarded by obstacles, so that the truthful seeker may be distinguished from the false. He frightens him with poverty and commands him to remain with causes and customary patterns—the most indecent disobedience in the eyes of the elect, for lofty aspiration disdains occupation with anything other than the divine Presence. God promises you who turn towards Him forgiveness of sins and concealment of faults. He covers your quality with His quality and your attribute with His attribute, bringing you to Himself through the bounty and generosity that come from Him to you, not through the struggle and endurance that come from you to Him. “Were it not for God’s bounty and mercy upon you, not one of you would ever become pure” [24:21]. God is expansive in generosity and goodness, knowing who is fit for bounty and grace."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>One fruit of renunciation and complete devotion is the coming of wisdom upon the servant’s tongue and heart, as He indicates next.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"One fruit of renunciation and complete devotion is the coming of wisdom upon the servant’s tongue and heart, as He indicates next."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C302.1","page":302,"ar":"البيت لعامر بن طفيل.","en":"The verse is by ʿĀmir ibn al-Ṭufayl.","anchor":"لمخلِفُ إيعادي ومنْجِزُ مَوْعدي"}],"english_characters":2582,"arabic_characters":1929},{"id":"q2-269","q":"2:269","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:269 · Wisdom and the union of power with wisdom","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 303–304","cairo":"I:303–304","source_page_first":303,"source_page_last":304,"ar":"يُؤْتِي الْحِكْمَةَ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَمَنْ يُؤْتَ الْحِكْمَةَ فَقَدْ أُوتِيَ خَيْراً كَثِيراً وَما يَذَّكَّرُ إِلاَّ أُولُوا الْأَلْبابِ (269)\nقال البيضاوي: الحكمة: تحقيق العلم وإتقان العمل. هـ. وقيل: هي سرعة الجواب وإصابة الصواب، وقيل:\nكل فضل جَزْلٍ من قول أو فعل.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله، يُؤْتِي الحق تعالى الْحِكْمَةَ مَنْ يَشاءُ من عباده، وهي التفقّه في الدين والتبصر في الأمور. قال صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «مَنْ يُرد اللهُ به خيراً يُفقِّهْه في الدين، ويُلهمْه رُشْدَه» ، وقيل: الحكمة: الإصابة في الرأي. وقيل: الفهم في كتاب الله. وقيل: الفهم عن الله. وَمَنْ يُؤْتَ الْحِكْمَةَ أي: أعطيها، فَقَدْ أُوتِيَ خَيْراً كَثِيراً لأنه حاز خيرَ الدارَيْن، ولا شك أن مَن حقَّق العلم بالله وبأحكامه، وأتقنَ العملَ بما أمره الله به، فقد صفا قلبه، وتطهر سره، فصار من أولي الألباب ولذلك قال عقبه: وَما يَذَّكَّرُ إِلَّا أُولُوا الْأَلْبابِ.\nالإشارة: الحكمة هي: شهود الذات مرتديةً بأنوار الصفات، وهي حقيقة المعرفة، ومن عرف الله هابه، ولذلك قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «رأس الحكمة مخافة الله» . وقيل: هي تجريد السر لورود الإلهام، وقيل: هي النور المفرق بين الوسواس والإلهام، وقيل: شهود الحق تعالى في جميع الأحوال. والتحقيق: أن الحكمة هي إبداع الشيء وإتقانه حتى يأتي على غاية الكمال، ويجري ذلك في العلم والعمل والحال والمعرفة.\nوقال القُشَيْري: الحكمة: أن يَحكم عليك خاطر الحق لا داعي الباطل، وأن تحكم قواهر الحق لا زواجر الشيطان.\nويقال: الحكمة: صواب الأمر، ويقال: هي ألا تغلب عليك رعونات البشرية، ومنْ لا حُكْم له على نَفْسِه لا حُكْمَ له على غيره. ويقال: الحكمة: موافقة أمر الله، والسفه: مخالفة أمره، ويقال: الحكمة شهود الحق، والسفه: شهود الغير.\nقاله المحشي.\nواعلم أن الصوفية، في اصطلاحهم، يُعبِّرون عن أسرار الذات بالقدرة، وعن أنوار الصفات- وهي ظهور آثارها- بالحكمة. فالوجودُ كله قائم بين الحكمة والقدرة، فالقدرة تُبرز الأشياء، والحكمة تَسترها. فربط الأشياء واقترانها بأسبابها تُسمى عندهم الحكمة، وإنفاذ الأمر وإظهاره يسمى القدرة، فمن وقف مع الحكمة حجب عن\nشهود القدرة، وكان محجوباً عن الله. ومن نفذ إلى شهود القدرة ولم يرتبط مع الأسباب والعوائد كان عارفاً محبوباً. فالعارف الكامل هو الذي جمع بين شهود القدرة وإقرار الحكمة، فأعطى كل ذي حق حقه، وَوَفَّى كلَّ ذي قسط قسطه، لكن يكون ذلك ذوقاً وكشفاً، لا علماً وتقليداً. وبالله تعالى التوفيق:\nثم رغّب فى الإخلاص، وحذّر من شوب الحظوظ فى النفقة، فقال:","quran_ar":"يُؤْتِي الْحِكْمَةَ مَنْ يَشاءُ وَمَنْ يُؤْتَ الْحِكْمَةَ فَقَدْ أُوتِيَ خَيْراً كَثِيراً وَما يَذَّكَّرُ إِلاَّ أُولُوا الْأَلْبابِ (269)","quran_en":"269. He gives wisdom to whom He wills. Whoever is given wisdom has been given abundant good. None takes heed except those possessed of understanding.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Knowledge realized and action perfected","html":"<p>Al-Bayḍāwī said: wisdom (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحكمة<\/bdi>) is realizing knowledge and perfecting action. Other definitions are: readiness in answering and correctness in judgment; or every substantial excellence of speech or deed.<\/p><p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: He gives wisdom to whom He wills among His servants—understanding religion and seeing clearly into affairs. The Prophet said: “Whomever God desires good for, He gives understanding in religion and inspires with right guidance.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-252\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B225]<\/a> Wisdom is also explained as sound judgment, understanding God’s Book, or understanding from God.<\/p><p>Whoever is given wisdom has received abundant good, for he has obtained the good of both abodes. One who realizes knowledge of God and His rulings and perfects the action He commands has surely purified his heart and cleansed his secret. He becomes one of those possessed of understanding (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أولو الألباب<\/bdi>). Hence the verse continues: “None takes heed except those possessed of understanding.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Al-Bayḍāwī said: wisdom ( الحكمة ) is realizing knowledge and perfecting action. Other definitions are: readiness in answering and correctness in judgment; or every substantial excellence of speech or deed. The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: He gives wisdom to whom He wills among His servants—understanding religion and seeing clearly into affairs. The Prophet said: “Whomever God desires good for, He gives understanding in religion and inspires with right guidance.” [B225] Wisdom is also explained as sound judgment, understanding God’s Book, or understanding from God. Whoever is given wisdom has received abundant good, for he has obtained the good of both abodes. One who realizes knowledge of God and His rulings and perfects the action He commands has surely purified his heart and cleansed his secret. He becomes one of those possessed of understanding ( أولو الألباب ). Hence the verse continues: “None takes heed except those possessed of understanding.”"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Wisdom as perfected perception and conduct","html":"<p>Wisdom is witnessing the Essence clothed in the lights of the attributes: the reality of gnosis. Whoever knows God stands in awe of Him. Hence the saying attributed to the Prophet: “The beginning of wisdom is fear of God.” Wisdom is also defined as stripping the secret free to receive inspiration; the light distinguishing satanic whispering from inspiration; or witnessing the Real Most High in every state.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-253\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B225]<\/a><\/p><p>The precise account is that wisdom means bringing a thing into being and perfecting it until it reaches the utmost completeness. This applies to knowledge, action, spiritual state and gnosis.<\/p><p>Al-Qushayrī said: wisdom is that an impulse from the Real should govern you, not a caller to falsehood; that the Real’s overpowering forces should rule, not Satan’s goads. Wisdom is also said to be correctness in affairs, or freedom from the dominance of human follies. Whoever has no rule over his own self has no rule over another. Wisdom is accord with God’s command, and folly is opposition to it. Wisdom is witnessing the Real, and folly is witnessing other than Him. The marginal commentator gives these sayings.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Wisdom is witnessing the Essence clothed in the lights of the attributes: the reality of gnosis. Whoever knows God stands in awe of Him. Hence the saying attributed to the Prophet: “The beginning of wisdom is fear of God.” Wisdom is also defined as stripping the secret free to receive inspiration; the light distinguishing satanic whispering from inspiration; or witnessing the Real Most High in every state. [B225] The precise account is that wisdom means bringing a thing into being and perfecting it until it reaches the utmost completeness. This applies to knowledge, action, spiritual state and gnosis. Al-Qushayrī said: wisdom is that an impulse from the Real should govern you, not a caller to falsehood; that the Real’s overpowering forces should rule, not Satan’s goads. Wisdom is also said to be correctness in affairs, or freedom from the dominance of human follies. Whoever has no rule over his own self has no rule over another. Wisdom is accord with God’s command, and folly is opposition to it. Wisdom is witnessing the Real, and folly is witnessing other than Him. The marginal commentator gives these sayings."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Power, wisdom and the complete gnostic","html":"<p>Know that, in their technical usage, the Sufis call the mysteries of the Essence “power” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القدرة<\/bdi>), and the lights of the attributes—the manifestation of their effects—“wisdom” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحكمة<\/bdi>). The whole of existence stands between wisdom and power. Power brings things forth; wisdom conceals them.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-254\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B226]<\/a><\/p><p>The connection of things and their association with causes is what they call wisdom. The execution of the command and its manifestation is called power. Someone who remains with wisdom is veiled from<\/p><p>witnessing power and is veiled from God. Someone who penetrates to witnessing power without remaining bound to causes and customary patterns is a beloved gnostic.<\/p><p>The complete gnostic unites witnessing power with acknowledging wisdom. He gives everyone possessing a right his right and renders everyone possessing a portion his portion. This must, however, be through taste and unveiling, not merely knowledge and imitation. And success is through God Most High.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Know that, in their technical usage, the Sufis call the mysteries of the Essence “power” ( القدرة ), and the lights of the attributes—the manifestation of their effects—“wisdom” ( الحكمة ). The whole of existence stands between wisdom and power. Power brings things forth; wisdom conceals them. [B226] The connection of things and their association with causes is what they call wisdom. The execution of the command and its manifestation is called power. Someone who remains with wisdom is veiled from witnessing power and is veiled from God. Someone who penetrates to witnessing power without remaining bound to causes and customary patterns is a beloved gnostic. The complete gnostic unites witnessing power with acknowledging wisdom. He gives everyone possessing a right his right and renders everyone possessing a portion his portion. This must, however, be through taste and unveiling, not merely knowledge and imitation. And success is through God Most High."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then encourages sincerity and warns against mixing expenditure with personal interests.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then encourages sincerity and warns against mixing expenditure with personal interests."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3153,"arabic_characters":2169}];
